($drill$Rhythm/Tempo Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$Progressively build from half-swings to full driver swings to lock in your personal tempo before a single ball goes sideways.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Butch Harmon's foundational observation — and the reason Rich Jones structures his PGA warm-ups the same way — is that golfers don't lose distance or accuracy from lack of effort; they lose it from tempo disruption. The driver is the longest club in the bag, so the backswing takes more time and the transition is the most likely place for the sequence to break down. Starting with controlled, rhythmic half-swings and building gradually gives the nervous system time to find the timing that belongs to *your* swing — not a generic "1-and-2" count, but the actual beat your body settles into when it isn't overloaded. Harmon cites the driver as the club most sensitive to a rushed transition: players feel they must "hit it harder" and snatch the club at the top before the lower body fires. The countdown progression bypasses that by building tempo as a sequence rather than conscious effort. **Who it helps most.** All levels as a pre-session reset, but beginners and casual players gain the most — they arrive with no reference tempo and immediately reach for a driver. Developing and competitive players use it to re-groove timing after layoffs or a swing change. **Setup.** A range bay, a driver, at least three balls per set. No aids. Normal tee height. **How.** Stage 1 — 3 swings at ~50%, smooth takeaway and full finish; hit one ball. Stage 2 — 3 swings at 70%, conscious of the transition pause; hit two balls. Stage 3 — 3 swings at 85%, no forcing; hit three balls. Stage 4 — full swings at the tempo you found at 85%; hit three to five. If a shot feels snatched, drop back a stage. **Time & frequency.** 8–10 minutes; every range session and as the first 10 minutes of an on-course warm-up. It's an on-ramp, not a drill to grind. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: finish balance held 2 seconds on 4 of 5 full swings. • Developing: ball-flight shape consistent on 4 of 5 at full speed. • Competitive: full swings match the shape and trajectory of the 85% swings, tempo indistinguishable. **Common mistakes.** Skipping to full swings; counting tempo aloud over the ball (rehearsal only); grinding on ball flight in stages 1–2 instead of feeling sequence; treating 50% as "easy" and still rushing the transition.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Butch Harmon (Golf Digest); Rich Jones, PGA (PGA.com)$drill$, 'https://golfdigest.com/story/butch-harmon-finding-your-rhythm-with-the-driver', 'oga', true),

($drill$Step-Through Speed$drill$,
 $drill$Step into the drive — Schauffele's running-start step-in or Harrington's gather-and-fire step-out — to train the weight transfer and lower-body lead that unlock real clubhead speed.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Two tour players teach the same idea through slightly different footwork: speed comes from transferring weight onto the lead side and letting the lower body lead the downswing, and a literal step makes that transfer impossible to fake. Most amateurs leak speed by staying flat-footed and spinning the upper body first — the over-the-top move that costs both speed and path. A step forces the chain to sequence from the ground up. Xander Schauffele frames his version as a running start: "Once I step, I can feel a lot of weight transfer from right to left… this helps the clubhead speed." Padraig Harrington frames his as pure athleticism: "be an athlete — when you throw a ball or swing a baseball bat you wouldn't do it with flat feet and no rotation." Both convert a static, arms-only swing into a dynamic, weight-shifting one. **Who it helps most.** Casual players whose weight stalls on the trail foot and developing players who pull or pull-hook from spinning out; competitive players use it to recover a lost release or add speed. Not for raw beginners — introduce after repeatable contact. **Setup.** Range only; the step needs space, off a normal driver tee. One bucket. **How — Schauffele step-in (primary).** Stand one full step behind the teed ball. As you start the backswing, step your lead foot forward toward the ball, then swing through impact with the weight moving onto the lead side. If walking into it feels unnatural, use Schauffele's alternative: keep the feet planted but lift the trail heel during the backswing to load the trail side, then press down hard into the lead foot coming down — the same chain without the step. **How — Harrington gather-step (variation).** From a normal address, step your lead foot back until it nearly meets the trail foot (feet almost together), make your backswing from there, then step the lead foot back out toward the target as you swing down. At first, let the result of the shot not matter — feel the athletic move. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week when working speed or tempo; at the start of driver work, not as a fatigue drill. Begin with 5 slow rehearsals of whichever step you pick, then 10 with a ball accepting erratic early contact, then alternate 1 step swing with 1 normal swing in sets of 10 pairs to transfer the feel. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: clean contact on 5 of 10; weight clearly on the lead foot at finish. • Developing: consistent contact with a draw/straight flight and a noticeable carry bump vs normal swings. • Competitive: step-swing flight matches normal shape; used as a rehearsal trigger when speed or timing is off. **Common mistakes.** Stepping too early so it becomes a lunge; a tiny tentative step (defeats the lower-body-lead effect); looking up to watch the ball; doing it on a mat where the feet can't move; rushing the gather-step downswing instead of letting the step sequence the swing.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Xander Schauffele (Golf.com); Padraig Harrington (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/driving/xander-schauffele-step-up-driver-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Upside-Down Whoosh$drill$,
 $drill$Flip the driver upside-down and swing until the loudest whoosh lands at the ball — your ears tell you exactly when you're releasing too early.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The whoosh is aerodynamic drag: the grip end, now the business end, cuts the air and its loudest point marks maximum club speed. When a golfer releases the wrists early — the "cast" that costs distance — peak speed happens before the ball and the whoosh peaks in the downswing. With correct timing the whoosh peaks at and just past the ball. Pete Styles and Titleist use it because it gives real-time, unconscious feedback: you don't think about lag, you swing and listen, and the brain adjusts to maximize the sound at the right spot. Titleist's kinematic-sequence research supports it — proper sequencing accelerates the club last. **Who it helps most.** Every level, because casting is universal and even competitive players develop it under pressure. Beginners get simple movement-based feedback without mechanics; developing/competitive players feel the difference between held-lag and their current pattern. **Setup.** Grip the club just below the head (hold the hosel) so the handle points down at address. No balls, no tee, no range — a living room or hotel corridor works. Five feet of clearance. **How.** Normal address posture; full backswing; swing down and through trying to make the loudest whoosh at and just past the ball. Don't flick the wrists to fake it — let the timing come from listening. If the whoosh peaks high in the downswing the release is early; past the ball, it's late. Center the peak at the ball + a foot of follow-through. 15–20 swings, pausing to register the sound. **Time & frequency.** 8–10 minutes; great as a pre-round warm-up and a no-facility corrective. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: peak audibly shifts toward the ball zone over the session. • Developing: peak at/past the ball on 8 of 10. • Competitive: peak at the same point every swing; used as a pre-round check. **Common mistakes.** Flicking the wrists to manufacture the sound; swinging too slowly to get a useful sound; stopping before the follow-through; using it too briefly (5 swings won't retrain the pattern).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Pete Styles (golf-info-guide.com); Titleist$drill$, 'https://golf-info-guide.com/drivers/use-whoosh-drill-to-hit-longer-drives/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Start-Line Intermediate Target$drill$,
 $drill$Aim the clubface at a spot a few feet ahead on the tee-shot line — not a target 200 yards away — to kill the biggest cause of open-face misalignment.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Human vision is unreliable for aiming a clubface perpendicular to a line beyond ~20 yards. Golfers setting up to a 220-yard target routinely finish 5–10° open or closed with no sense of it. The intermediate-target method — used on tour and at PGA schools — gives a reference only a few feet ahead (a divot, a discolored patch, a tee), a task the visual system can do accurately. The face is aimed at something 2–4 feet away; the full target is handled through feet and body parallel-left. This trains a routine, not a swing mechanic. **Who it helps most.** High-handicappers whose drives leak right (usually alignment, not swing) improve quickest. Developing and competitive players use intermediate targeting as the backbone of their routine, especially under pressure, because it removes a variable at address. **Setup.** Range, driver, full bucket. From behind the ball pick a spot 2–4 feet ahead on the start line; drop a broken tee if no natural marker. A full-length alignment stick on the line confirms it for the first sessions. **How.** Walk in from directly behind the target line. Set the face perpendicular to the ball-to-intermediate-target line first. Build the stance around it — feet, hips, shoulders parallel-left. Glance at the full target once, return to the intermediate target or ball, swing. Hit 5, walk back behind each, verify with the stick. Sets of 10 with a behind-ball check before each. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes every range session — it's routine-building, not a corrective to retire; competitive players use it on every shot. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: face visibly square to the intermediate target on setup (video/partner confirms). • Developing: start line matches intended line on 7 of 10. • Competitive: intermediate-target selection automatic; no starts right of the line. **Common mistakes.** Aiming the body at the intermediate target (it should be parallel-left); picking a target too far away (recreates the perception problem); skipping the walk-in; abandoning the routine under pressure.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{start_line}', 'blocked',
 $drill$PGA.com$drill$, 'https://pga.com/story/3-fun-drills-to-dial-in-your-driving-chipping-and-putting', 'oga', true),

($drill$Towel Attack-Angle$drill$,
 $drill$Place a folded towel six inches behind the teed ball so a steep, descending attack angle gets punished — and you learn to launch the driver up.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Alison Curdt's drill targets the root of most amateur distance loss with a driver: a descending blow. Irons are struck slightly down, so golfers apply the same pattern to a teed-up ball that should be hit 3–5° on the upswing. A descending driver strike adds dynamic loft, spikes spin (often 4,000+ rpm versus an optimal ~2,000–2,500 for faster swing speeds — slower swings want a bit more), and kills carry. A towel placed behind the ball is a physical obstacle: descend and you clip it; sweep up (low point behind the ball) and it stays untouched. Tactile, penalty-based feedback that changes the pattern without the golfer needing to understand attack angle. **Who it helps most.** Beginners and casual players with a habitual driver "chop" — the most common distance-loss pattern. Developing players (break-90 range) with inconsistent launch and too much spin. Not needed for competitive players already calibrated on a launch monitor. **Setup.** Range with a tee. Fold a towel to ~4 inches high, 8 inches wide; place it flat ~5–6 inches directly behind the teed ball, centered on the ball line. Standard driver tee height. **How.** Address and make a full swing. No towel contact = low point at/before the towel and an ascending strike. A towel strike = you descended. Feel "sweeping the ball off the tee" rather than hitting down; Curdt's cue is the butt of the club pointing more at the ground at address (ball forward, weight slightly back). 5 swings at 80% to calibrate, then sets of 10 at full speed. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly until the descending pattern is gone, then retire; revive for a session if it returns. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: no towel contact on 5 of 10. • Developing: no contact on 8 of 10; visibly higher launch and carry. • Competitive: not the audience; if used, zero contact. **Common mistakes.** Towel too far back (room to descend and still miss it); teeing too low (encourages descending); shoving the ball so far forward the aim shifts (keep normal ball position, change the swing); quitting after the first few contacts instead of reading them.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Alison Curdt, PGA Master Pro (Golf Tips Mag)$drill$, 'https://golftipsmag.com/instruction/driving/try-towel-drill-for-longer-drives/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Headcover Path$drill$,
 $drill$Set a headcover just outside the toe at address to train an inside path and kill the over-the-top move behind pulls and slices.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Jonathan Yarwood's Titleist drill builds a physical gate that makes an over-the-top, outside-in path impossible without hitting the headcover. A pull or slice with the driver is almost always path: the club approaches from outside the line and glances across the ball, creating sidespin. The headcover sits exactly where an outside-in clubhead would travel; hitting it is instant, unambiguous punishment. The correct inside-to-square path threads the clubhead inside the headcover, past the ball, and out to the right of target early in the follow-through. It teaches path without an abstract plane image and works for visual learners (they see the obstacle) and proprioceptive learners (they feel the change). **Who it helps most.** Developing players with a consistent slice or pull see immediate feedback; competitive players use it to correct an over-the-top pattern that creeps in under pressure and as a pre-round shallowing drill. **Setup.** Range, driver, one headcover (a small iron headcover is less intimidating than a driver one). Tee normally; place the headcover ~6 inches outside the toe (right of the head for a righty), level with the ball, on the ground or a low tee. Larger gap for beginners to the drill, tighten as path improves. **How.** Address with the headcover in place; full swing. Untouched = neutral-to-inside path; struck = outside-in. Don't steer around it — make full, committed swings and adjust by outcome. Thought: trace the inside of a barrel from top to impact. 3 rehearsal swings without a ball, then 10 balls; two sets. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on path; pre-round (3–5 swings, then remove) is a competitive standard. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: headcover untouched on 7 of 10; flight moving from pull/slice to straight or slight draw. • Competitive: untouched every swing; normal shape maintained. **Common mistakes.** Headcover too far outside (room to be outside-in and miss it); casting early to get inside (clears it but produces fat contact); gripping tighter from fear of hitting it; using a bulky driver headcover that creates needless fear.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Jonathan Yarwood (Titleist)$drill$, 'https://titleist.com/videos/instruction/driver-headcover-drill', 'oga', true),

($drill$Path Gate$drill$,
 $drill$Plant two alignment sticks in a narrow channel and swing through them to enforce a neutral path and square face — no forgiveness for sloppy entry angles.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The path gate turns swing-path feedback from abstract (plane diagrams) into a physical binary: thread the needle or clip a stick. PGA.com and MyGolfSpy both favor stick gates over mirror or video work because the consequence is immediate and in your periphery during the swing, not reviewed afterward. The gate works on two mechanisms at once: it constrains the clubhead's entry angle (path) and requires a square face to pass cleanly through the narrow opening. A wide gate catches only the worst swings; a tight gate punishes 2–3° of path deviation — the precision that separates 15-handicap dispersion from 5-handicap dispersion with a driver. **Who it helps most.** Developing players with variable path (sometimes inside-out, sometimes over-the-top) who can't self-diagnose; competitive players use a tighter gate as a precision tool and a tour warm-up staple. Not for beginners — the gate needs consistent contact to give useful feedback. **Setup.** Two alignment sticks (in the ground or mat grooves) forming a 6–8-inch channel parallel to the target line, positioned so the impact zone passes through the center — one stick just outside the toe-line, one just inside the heel-line, at or near ground level. Tee the ball in the gate center; narrow as accuracy improves. **How.** Address inside the gate; full swing. Clean pass = path on-line and face square enough; a toe-side clip = outside-in; a heel-side clip = an over-aggressive inside-out. 5 slow-motion practice swings (no ball) to confirm you can pass it, then 10 balls at full speed. Note which side you clip — the pattern reveals your tendency. Alternate 1 gate swing with 1 normal swing. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week when path is the focus; afterward use periodically as a check. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: gate untouched on 6 of 10 full-speed swings; clipping only one side (a consistent tendency to fix). • Competitive: untouched on 9 of 10 at a tight 5-inch setting; shape matches intent. **Common mistakes.** Gate too wide (misses the 3–4° errors that cost 20 yards offline); aiming the gate left of target (makes a correct path look outside-in); steering through instead of committing; not alternating with free swings (the gate becomes a crutch).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$PGA.com; MyGolfSpy$drill$, 'https://pga.com/story/the-3-best-alignment-stick-drills-to-play-better-golf', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tipping-Point Speed Test$drill$,
 $drill$Push driver speed up until your pattern breaks, then back off one step — that edge is your trainable ceiling, where speed gains happen without losing control.$drill$,
 20, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Michael Breed's idea is that every golfer has a "tipping point" — the swing speed at which the movement pattern degrades and dispersion explodes. Most players practice well below it (safe, no gains) or randomly above it (inconsistent, grooves breakdown). The method is structured: find the ceiling by escalating effort, identify where the pattern breaks, and train just below it. At that edge the nervous system is challenged enough to adapt — the "desirable difficulty" zone — without reinforcing breakdown. On a launch monitor, ball speed and smash factor (ball speed ÷ club speed) flag the tip-over objectively: smash drops below ~1.44 or carry variance spikes. **Who it helps most.** Developing players plateaued on distance — either below their ceiling (no overload) or above it (grooving breakdown). Competitive players quantify their trainable ceiling and track it across a season. Requires a launch monitor/sim; it doesn't work at a feedback-free range. **Setup.** Launch monitor or sim with club/ball speed; 20–25 balls; driver on tee. Baseline: 10 swings at a comfortable, repeatable effort; note average ball speed and smash. **How.** Round 1: +~10% effort, 5 balls, record average ball speed and smash. Round 2: +another ~10%, 5 balls. Repeat until smash consistently drops below ~1.44 or carry varies more than ~20 yards ball-to-ball. The round before degradation is your working ceiling; train there for the rest of the session (10+ balls) at controlled 100% commitment, not a lunge. The goal is training the pattern at the edge, not proving the edge exists. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, once a week in a speed phase; not daily — the nervous system needs recovery. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: smash 1.44+ maintained at a higher effort than the starting session, measured 4 weeks apart. • Competitive: ceiling up 2–3 mph ball speed over a 4-week block; smash 1.48+ at the working ceiling. **Common mistakes.** Jumping straight to max effort (skips the data); a range with no monitor (no objective tip-over); stopping at the first bad shot rather than a statistical pattern; treating it as feel rather than a data drill.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Michael Breed (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://golfdigest.com/story/simple-truth-about-practice-why-working-on-simulator-swing-beats-range', 'oga', true),

($drill$Fairway Target Game$drill$,
 $drill$Simulate a round of tee shots to a virtual fairway that shrinks as you improve — competitive pressure without leaving the range.$drill$,
 20, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Jon Sherman's Practical Golf research and MassGolf's range games reach the same insight: practicing driver by swinging freely and watching shape rarely improves on-course dispersion, because the range has no consequences. The fairway game adds a scoring consequence and a shrinking target mirroring a real tee shot — a fairway is ~30–40 yards wide and your driver must fit. Sherman's data shows most casual golfers have 40+ yard dispersion and don't know it because they never measure. The game makes dispersion visible under pressure; the MassGolf shrinking variant adds a progression (hit it three straight, it narrows). **Who it helps most.** Casual players with no reference for acceptable dispersion; developing players who know they must keep it in play but never trained to a corridor; competitive players use a tighter starting fairway (25 yards) for pressure. All benefit from the one-shot-per-hole commitment. **Setup.** Range targets/markers at driver distance, or set the fairway width on a sim. If no markers, use two sticks or landmarks (flags, buckets) to define a 40-yard corridor at ~220–250 yards. Score: fairway = 1, miss = 0; record every shot. **How.** Play 9 "holes." Each: pick a specific aim line (vary it to simulate doglegs), run your full routine, hit one driver, record hit/miss and miss side. Tally after 9. Hit 7 of 9 → narrow the corridor 5 yards next round; under 5 of 9 → keep it until you hit 7+. Track miss patterns: same side every time is path/face information. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes (~18 tee shots + routines + recording), once a week as an assessment; add sessions during a driving-accuracy block. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 5 of 9 in a 40-yard corridor consistently. • Developing: 7 of 9 in 35 yards; miss pattern moving from one-sided to random. • Competitive: 7 of 9 in 25 yards; can simulate course shapes (aim at the right edge to play a fade to center). **Common mistakes.** Skipping the routine (turns it back into range-bashing); not tracking miss side (the direction is half the picture); too-generous corridor (no improvement signal); multiple balls per "hole" (one-and-done is what golf asks).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$MassGolf; Jon Sherman (Practical Golf)$drill$, 'https://practical-golf.com/driver-dispersion', 'oga', true),

($drill$Driver Dispersion Mapping$drill$,
 $drill$Plot all 20 of your drives — including the bad ones — to build a true dispersion picture that should inform every tee-shot decision you make.$drill$,
 25, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Jon Sherman's core argument: most golfers manage tee shots based on a fictional "when I hit it well" trajectory. Real course management needs the actual distribution — where drives land across a representative 20-shot sample, blow-ups included. The mapping protocol produces that: a scatter that reveals whether your dispersion is symmetric or biased, how wide your 1-sigma corridor really is, and whether your miss is a predictable working shot or a low-frequency disaster. A player who knows their dispersion is 30 yards wide with a slight right bias can aim 10 yards left of center on a 40-yard fairway and never miss left. Without the map they aim center and lose half their drives right. **Who it helps most.** Competitive players (break-80 to scratch) leaking shots to course-management errors rather than swing errors. It's a measurement exercise — it presupposes enough consistency that 20 shots are a pattern, not chaos. Not for beginners/casual players whose dispersion is too large to be actionable. **Setup.** Range with yardage + lateral markers, or a launch monitor/sim; a printed grid or notes app. Fix one aim line for all 20 shots; full routine on every ball. **How.** Commit to one aim line for all 20. Hit each with full effort and routine — no do-overs. Record each: carry, and left/right deviation from the aim line in yards. After 20, find (1) your 1-sigma band width (the middle ~14 shots); (2) your miss tendency (average deviation; positive = right bias); (3) your blow-up rate (shots beyond 2× the typical miss). Repeat monthly or before a big round. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes, once a month as a benchmark — not weekly. The training response goes into the Fairway Target Game or path work. **Success metric, by level.** • Break 80: 1-sigma corridor ≤30 yards; an identifiable (non-random) miss pattern. • Break 70s: corridor ≤20 yards; miss tendency within 5 yards of the aim line. • Scratch: corridor ≤15 yards; blow-ups (beyond 30 yards offline) under 10%. **Common mistakes.** Changing the aim line mid-session (invalidates the data); excluding bad shots (the blow-ups are the most valuable data); mapping once and never again (dispersion shifts with technique); using the data to justify aggressive lines rather than to calibrate conservative ones (Sherman's point: it should make you more conservative).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,course_management}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Jon Sherman (Practical Golf)$drill$, 'https://practical-golf.com/driver-dispersion', 'oga', true),

($drill$Wedge Distance Ladder (Pelz 3×4)$drill$,
 $drill$Map a personal carry for three swing lengths across each of your wedges — Pelz's 3×4 system — so every scoring-zone distance is a known number, not a guess.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Most amateur wedge misses are distance errors, not direction errors — the ball finishes left or right because it went short or long first. Controlling carry needs a repeatable reference for swing *size*, not speed manipulation. Dave Pelz established this in the Short Game Bible with a clock-face metaphor and formalized it as the 3×4 system: three finesse backswing lengths (~7:30/hip height, 9:00/lead-arm parallel, 10:30/near-full turn) across four wedges (pitching, gap, sand, lob) = twelve known carries. Michael Breed anchors the swing-size to shoulder rotation rather than arm height, because your lead shoulder is visible in peripheral vision — a built-in checkpoint. Phil Mickelson has credited the 3×4 system for the wedge distance control behind his Masters wins. Pre-round, the same matrix doubles as calibration: conditions shift carry day to day, so hitting it tells you whether your 9:00 sand wedge flies 82 or 78 today. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who already make consistent contact but miss too many greens inside 100 yards because their partial-swing distances are fuzzy, and anyone who plays varying conditions, courses, or elevations. Presupposes ball-first contact; beginners build that first. **Setup.** Your wedges (pitching/gap/sand/lob) and an open bay; a flag, towel, or marker at the target. Range flags, GPS, or a launch monitor to confirm carry — estimation isn't accurate enough for a gapping matrix. **How.** Working one wedge at a time (highest-lofted to lowest, stay consistent): three balls at 7:30, three at 9:00, three at 10:30, logging the average carry for each. Nine per wedge, thirty-six total → twelve numbers. Use shoulder turn, not wrist hinge, as the size cue. Ladder refinement: pick one set (e.g., the 9:00 sand wedge), hit your stock carry, then one deliberately short and one over, to feel each rung's edges. Write the twelve carries on a yardage card and use it when you're between clubs. Calm and deliberate — calibration, not grinding. **Time & frequency.** ~20 minutes for the full twelve-distance matrix every 2–3 weeks (and after a gear or swing change); one wedge's three lengths is a ~5-minute pre-round warm-up, a trimmed 3×2 (two wedges) about six. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 3 consecutive shots per length within 8 yards of the same average; can name a 40–100-yard target and pick the swing-length + wedge combo within 5 yards. • Competitive: all twelve carries consistent within 4–5 yards; the card matches on-course behavior within half a club. **Common mistakes.** Chasing distance with speed instead of swing length (defeats the system); using wrist-hinge instead of shoulder turn as the cue (less repeatable); hitting only full swings and calling it gapping (leaves the finesse range unmapped); logging your longest ball as your distance; skipping the shortest length (it owns the 40–60-yard shots, the most common full-miss range); using total distance instead of carry (carry varies far more with conditions).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Dave Pelz (Short Game Bible); Michael Breed (Golf Digest); Golf.com$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/approach-shots/wedge-shots-distance-control-ladder-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$DJ Wedge Combine$drill$,
 $drill$Hit half, three-quarter, and full shots with each wedge and demand five straight carries inside a tight yardage window — Dustin Johnson's pre-round tightening protocol.$drill$,
 30, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dustin Johnson sat just inside the top 40 in proximity from inside 100 yards before overhauling his practice; after adopting a combine-style, launch-monitor-tracked approach he climbed to third on Tour in that stat within a year. The combine format works because it applies a pass/fail performance window: set a window, hit until you achieve it, move on — unlike "hitting a bucket of wedges" with no criteria and no pressure. The five-within-a-window target also forces you to notice dispersion, not just average carry — a single outlier that blows the window flags a sequencing or tempo issue. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with established wedge technique who want to convert known carries into repeatable execution under self-imposed pressure. Needs at least estimated carry data; a GPS range or launch monitor helps. **Setup.** Three wedges (gap, 56°, 60°). Launch monitor ideal; else a range with known yardages and visual estimation. Window: 5 yards for developing, 3 for competitive. **How.** Lob wedge first: half swings until five consecutive carries fall within your window; record average. Then three-quarter, then full. Move to sand, then gap — same three lengths each. On an outlier, reset the *window counter* (not the whole set) and continue. ~45–54 shots; experienced players finish in 30 minutes. **Time & frequency.** 25–35 minutes; pre-round once a week or before any competitive start; one wedge's three lengths (~10 min) on off-days to maintain calibration. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: all nine sets (3 wedges × 3 lengths) with five-ball windows ≤8 yards. • Competitive: windows ≤5 yards for all nine; knows each carry within 2 yards without a monitor. **Common mistakes.** Resetting the whole set instead of the window counter; making compensatory swings to fill a window rather than diagnosing the outlier; skipping half-swing sets (those 40–60-yard shots are the highest-leverage scoring range).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$GolfWRX; Golf.com$drill$, 'https://golfwrx.com/509476/improve-your-wedge-distance-control-with-dustin-johnsons-unique-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Ball-First Contact (Towel & Tee)$drill$,
 $drill$Use a towel behind the ball, a towel in front, or a tee just forward of it to groove the descending, ball-then-turf strike every iron needs.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tour pros transfer significant mass to the lead side before impact, which pulls the low point of the swing arc *forward* of the ball and makes a descending, ball-then-turf strike — the divot starts where the ball sat or just ahead. Most recreational players do the opposite: they stall their rotation and try to "help" the ball up, pinning the low point behind the ball, which produces thin or fat contact. Three placement constraints attack that fault and make the fix impossible to fake. The towel behind the ball (HackMotion) is a deterrence cue — bottom out early and you clip it, instant proof the low point is too far back. A second towel in front (Cameron McCormick, Jordan Spieth's longtime coach) is a confirmation cue — you must clip the front towel as the club passes, proving the handle and lead side moved through the zone (McCormick's image: the handle looks like it's over the front towel at impact). The tee in front (Indiana Golf Journal) is a completion cue — a tee pressed in just ahead of the ball must be knocked out, proving the club reached past ball-center on a downward path; if it stays, you scooped. **Who it helps most.** Beginners, casual, and developing players who chronically thin, fat, scoop, or take a divot behind the ball (or none); developing players in a contact regression. Especially useful for players who understand ball-first contact cognitively but can't yet produce it. Not needed for players who already strike ball-before-turf. **Setup.** Grass range or practice tee, a 7 or 8 iron (the steeper iron attack angle is the point — not a wedge, not a wood). Pick one constraint: (a) a dense, folded towel with its near edge ~1.5 clubhead-lengths (2–3 inches) behind the ball; (b) add a second towel ~2 clubhead-lengths in front, ball in the gap; or (c) press a tee into the turf just ahead of the ball, tip at the ball-forward edge, angled slightly toward the target, top just above the turf. **How.** Make a normal full swing, watching the constraint, not ball flight. Towel-behind: a clean miss = low point at/ahead of the ball; clipping it = low point too far back. Two-towel: miss the back, clip the front as the club passes (never reaching the front = the body stalled and the hands flipped). Tee-in-front: tee knocked out = ball-first; tee still standing = you scooped. 10 shots per constraint, then two more sets of 10. Once "miss the back / clip the front / knock the tee" feels automatic, replicate it without looking. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week until 8 of 10 are clean; then a monthly check or when fat/thin/scooping returns. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 5–6 of 10 clean. • Developing: 8 of 10 clear the back (and clip the front / knock the tee). • Competitive: remedial diagnostic only — 10 for 10 before moving on. **Common mistakes.** Towel too far back (passes with a shallow miss); decelerating from fear of the back towel (makes fat contact more likely); slamming down to force the tee out (a dig, not a divot); watching the constraint instead of a normal focus point at impact; starting with a lofted wedge because it's easier (use a 7–8 iron, which exposes the fault); quitting the moment results improve (needs 2–3 weeks to hold under pressure).$drill$,
