 $drill$Cameron McCormick (Golf.com); HackMotion; Indiana Golf Journal$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/approach-shots/cameron-mccormick-tour-pro-iron-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Pump & Pause Sequencing$drill$,
 $drill$Interrupt the downswing at the halfway point once or twice before completing the swing to feel correct transition sequence and prevent early release.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Butch Harmon's pump drill addresses a top iron-consistency killer: casting, where the wrists unhinge and the clubhead overtakes the hands before impact. The fix makes transition a conscious, interruptible process — take the club to the top, start down, stop when the hands reach halfway, pump back to the top, pause again, then complete and hit. You experience the correct sequence in slow motion: lower body leads, arms drop from the inside, the club lags. Tiger Woods used a related stop-start method (pausing at the top and halfway down) to feel the same check. All versions isolate transition and make casting mechanically impossible during the paused reps. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who go over the top, cast early, or lose lag before impact; players with alternating thins and fats (a sequencing problem). Competitive players rarely need it unless recovering from a swing change. **Setup.** Mid-iron (7 or 8) on grass or mat; a face-on mirror or video helps verify the halfway-down position (hands at hip height, shaft roughly parallel). **How.** Normal address; full backswing; start down and freeze when hands reach hip height; hold one second; pump back to the top; pause again at halfway; then swing through and hit. That's the two-pump version. Alternative single-pause: a three-quarter backswing, pause one second, complete and hit. 10 drill reps, then 10 normal shots retaining the lag/sequence feel; alternate 10/10/10/10. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week in a contact phase; then normal practice with occasional pump sets as a reset. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: contact improves from mostly thin/fat to majority center-face within three sessions. • Developing: can pump twice, hold each pause, and still strike cleanly; normal shots show a divot in front of the ball. **Common mistakes.** Rushing the pause because it feels unnatural (the discomfort is the point); pausing too low near impact (no lag left to preserve); using a driver (the descending sequence is iron/wedge-specific); pumping as fast as a normal swing (kills the slow-motion benefit).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80}', '{contact,tempo}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Butch Harmon (Golf Digest); Tiger Woods (Golf365)$drill$, 'https://golfdigest.com/story/improve-your-iron-consistency-with-one-of-butch-harmons-favorite-drills', 'oga', true),

($drill$Body-Rotation Wedge (Alignment Stick)$drill$,
 $drill$Hold an alignment stick as a shaft extension against your lead ribcage to feel whether body rotation — not hand action — powers your wedges.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Justin Rose's wedge philosophy is that distance and contact consistency come from body-driven motion, not hands and wrists manipulating the face. When the body stalls and the hands take over — the most common amateur wedge fault — face angle becomes unpredictable and contact inconsistent. The stick extension makes the fault unmistakable: grip it as an extension of the handle so it runs along your lead forearm and rests against your lead ribcage; make a wedge swing. If the hands flip or the body stops, the stick jams your ribcage; if the body keeps turning with the hands ahead of the ball through impact, the stick clears your side. No interpretation required — it either hits you or it doesn't. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose wedge contact gets inconsistent under pressure, especially those who flip or decelerate on short shots; anyone whose finesse-wedge distance control wanders because the hands are doing the work (same "swing," different distances day to day). **Setup.** Hold an alignment stick against the grip so it extends past the butt along the forearms, lightly touching the lead ribcage at address. Any wedge; start with your most-used scoring wedge. **How.** Half-to-three-quarter swings, focused on the stick clearing the ribcage through impact. Start with slow practice swings (no ball) until five consecutive clear without contact; then a ball — 10 with the stick, 10 without, replicating the clear. Rose progresses from hip-height finesse swings up to fuller wedges in one session. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week in a technique phase; competitive players, 5–10 minutes as a pre-round check when wedge contact feels off. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 7 of 10 stick-attached swings clear the ribcage. • Competitive: 10 of 10 clear; normal shots show a consistent forward-leaning shaft at impact with no flip. **Common mistakes.** Holding the stick loosely so it drifts off the ribcage (nullifies feedback); consciously "keeping the stick away" with tension (counterproductive); swinging only to hip height to avoid the difficulty (drill it at the lengths where the fault appears); ignoring a jab and continuing — stop, reset, find the cause.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{contact,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Justin Rose (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://golfdigest.com/story/justin-rose-how-to-control-your-wedges', 'oga', true),

($drill$Syrupy Tempo Wedge$drill$,
 $drill$Make a lower-lofted wedge match a higher-lofted club's distance by smoothing your arm speed — not swinging easier — to build fluid, repeatable wedge tempo.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Parker McLachlin (Short Game Chef) notes most golfers create distance variety by swinging harder or easier — tempo-inconsistent, causing contact fluctuation. His syrupy drill uses a structural constraint to teach the right lever: arm speed managed smoothly throughout, not decelerated at impact. "Syrupy" describes a fluid, evenly-paced swing in both directions — not slow, but even, the way pouring syrup looks different from pouring water. Asking you to match a shorter club's carry with a longer one forces a smooth arm speed below maximum power with no jerkiness. As he puts it: "I don't want you to necessarily slow down on the way through, I just want it to be syrupy all the way back and through." Decelerating at impact — the instinctive way most golfers hit soft shots — opens the face and moves contact down the face. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players with inconsistent wedge distance, especially those who hit partial wedges thin or have a big gap between "careful" and "normal" tempo. Not for players who already own a smooth tempo (use Pelz/DJ structures instead). **Setup.** Two wedges with a meaningful loft gap (e.g., 56° and 52°, or 60° and 56°); an open bay to see flight. **How.** Step 1: 5 full swings with the higher-lofted club; note the average carry (your target). Step 2: switch to the lower-lofted club; match that carry — the lower loft wants to fly farther, so the only way to match is to smoothly slow arm speed. 5 shots, fluid takeaway to finish. Step 3: alternate clubs one shot each, keeping syrupy feel on both; three sets of 10 alternating. The goal is grooving the deceleration-free tempo, not perfectly matching the carry every time. **Time & frequency.** 12–15 minutes, 2–3×/week; excellent pre-round because it activates rhythm before full swings. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: lower-lofted club within 10 yards of the higher-lofted carry using smoother tempo, not a shorter swing. • Developing: within 5 yards; visibly smooth tempo with no lunge or deceleration on 8 of 10. **Common mistakes.** Shortening the backswing to hit the lower club shorter (a different skill, defeats the purpose); decelerating through the ball; rushing both clubs at full distance without comparing carries; choosing clubs ≤4° apart (gap too small to feel).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80}', '{contact,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Parker McLachlin / Short Game Chef (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/syrupy-drill-improved-ball-striking-distance-parker-mclachlin/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Nine Windows$drill$,
 $drill$Work through all nine combinations of three trajectories and three shot shapes with one iron to build complete ball-flight command.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods made this the backbone of his iron warm-up because it forces complete ownership of ball flight rather than grooving one shape. The matrix — three trajectory rows (low/medium/high) across three shape columns (draw/straight/fade) — equals nine windows. Working opposite corners (e.g., high draw to low fade) keeps the swing balanced and prevents over-practicing one compensation. As Woods put it: "It is three different trajectories... Then... whether you are going to cut them or draw them. Those are your nine windows." On the course you choose the window the situation demands — the slope behind the pin, the wind, the miss you can't afford — so practicing all nine means none is foreign under pressure. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with functional iron mechanics who want to expand shot-making. Not a contact drill — it presupposes consistent center-face contact. Competitive players use it as a warm-up protocol; developing players use it to find which windows expose technique gaps. **Setup.** One mid-iron (6 or 7); a target 150–180 yards out; a 3×3 tic-tac-toe grid on paper to track windows. **How.** Work across the matrix or corner to corner. Low: ball half-back, stand slightly closer (steeper, de-lofted). High: ball forward, hands finish high. Draw: face closed to path, full rotation through. Fade: face slightly open, soft hands through. One shot per window, tracked on the grid; note which needed the most attempts — Woods says those reveal your current limitation, so concentrate follow-up there. One pass = 9 shots; a full warm-up is 2–3 passes (18–27). **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes, 2–3 passes; a pre-round warm-up for competitive rounds. Off-season, a dedicated session to build new windows; low fade and high draw are typically hardest for righties. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: all nine hit with intentional shape (deliberate, not perfect). • Competitive: all nine matching intent within a club-width of dispersion; can identify and execute the window the situation demands. **Common mistakes.** Only hitting your natural shape at different heights instead of truly changing shape; treating each window as a separate swing rather than small setup levers (ball position, stance, finish height); wrong iron (a wedge makes trajectory windows too hard to read; a 3-iron makes everything a battle); skipping uncomfortable windows instead of using them as diagnostics.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory,dispersion,start_line}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Tiger Woods (GolfWRX/TaylorMade)$drill$, 'https://golfwrx.com/664080/tiger-woods-breaks-down-his-famous-nine-window-warm-up-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Three-Ball Trajectory$drill$,
 $drill$Hit three iron shots — normal stance distance, then closer, then farther from the ball — to feel how stance distance controls trajectory without changing your swing.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Titleist's drill isolates a setup variable most golfers never test: distance from the ball, i.e. how upright or flat the shaft sits at address. Conventional teaching changes ball position (forward/back) for trajectory, but stance distance is an equally powerful, less-discussed lever. Closer to the ball steepens the shaft, promotes a more upright plane, and produces a higher, softer flight; farther away flattens the shaft, encourages a shallower, sweeping plane, and produces a lower, more penetrating flight. Because these are setup adjustments rather than in-swing manipulations, they're more repeatable than consciously changing trajectory mid-swing. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who make consistent contact but have a one-flight iron vocabulary; especially valuable for players who fight wind, face elevated pins, or compete on firm, fast greens where trajectory determines proximity. **Setup.** A mid-iron (6 or 7); a target at the right distance. Mark your normal stance with a tee or use foot width as a reference; you move your feet relative to the ball, not the ball. **How.** Shot 1 — Normal: stock stance, stock shot, note trajectory. Shot 2 — Close: feet ~2 inches closer (shaft more upright), same swing, observe a higher, softer flight. Shot 3 — Far: feet ~2 inches farther (shaft flatter, hands lower), same swing, observe a lower, boring flight. Five sets of these three, noting the differences. Small reliable shifts, not exaggerations. **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes, 2–3×/week during trajectory work; once both non-stock flights are reproducible, a pre-round check (one set) before courses where trajectory matters. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: reliably identifies which setup gives which trajectory (predicts before hitting). • Competitive: can call "high"/"low" and execute via setup alone; close-stance lands 5+ yards shorter, far-stance runs out farther. **Common mistakes.** Compensating with the swing to overcome the setup change; moving ball position instead of stance distance (a different variable); moving 6+ inches (off-center contact instead of clean trajectory change); not taking a practice swing after each shift to feel the new shaft angle.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Titleist$drill$, 'https://titleist.com/videos/instruction/three-swing-training-control-trajectory-by-adjusting-your-distance-from-the-ball', 'oga', true),

($drill$Know Your Iron Distances$drill$,
 $drill$Hit ten carry-focused shots with every iron, log the averages, and build the personal yardage card that makes every approach a known quantity.$drill$,
 45, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Digest's iron-distance protocol rests on one insight: your real *average* carry, not your *best* carry, is what matters. Most amateurs carry a mental yardage 10–15 yards optimistic because it came from their best shot — the one they hit once in six. That optimism drives chronic short-siding and back-of-green misses. The fix is simple but uncomfortable: hit 10 balls per club under no-pressure range conditions, discard obvious mishits, and average the rest. A proper gapping session also reveals overlaps (two clubs within 5 yards) and gaps (a 20-yard jump), both of which affect equipment and strategy. **Who it helps most.** Every level, but the relative gain is highest for beginners and casual players playing with completely wrong assumptions. Developing players often know their scoring irons but guess on long irons. Competitive players use it as post-fitting verification and seasonal recalibration. **Setup.** A range with measured flags or a GPS/launch monitor; a notepad/phone; your full iron set from shortest scoring wedge to longest iron/hybrid. Plan 45 minutes: ~10 balls/club, 7–8 clubs, plus rest. **How.** Start with the gap or pitching wedge: 10 full, committed, *normal* stock swings (not your hardest). Note each carry, ignoring roll. Discard the two outliers (one thin, one flushed), average the remaining 8 — that's your carry. Move up a club; repeat. Build a table (club/carry/total). Gaps >15 yards suggest a loft gap or missing hybrid; overlaps <8 yards mean a redundant club. Write the carries on a card; reference it before every approach until automatic. **Time & frequency.** 45 minutes for a full 7-club session; once each season start, after gear or swing changes, and after a multi-week break (cold air cuts carry 5–8%). **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: a written card for at least 5 clubs, used instead of guessing. • Developing: full 7-club table within 5 yards of accurate; can recite each carry. • Competitive: full bag mapped incl. wedge swing lengths; recalibrated after conditions/gear; no club-selection uncertainty 60–180 yards. **Common mistakes.** Swinging your hardest to maximize the number (unreproducible on course); counting total distance (incl. roll) instead of carry; using range balls without noting they fly 5–10% shorter; doing it tired/rushed (skews the average); relying on memory instead of logging (drifts optimistic within two weeks).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Golf Digest$drill$, 'https://golfdigest.com/story/golf-digest-how-to-ways-to-dial-your-iron-distances', 'oga', true),

($drill$5-Target Random Wedge Test$drill$,
 $drill$Pick five random wedge distances, commit one shot each with a full routine, and score pass/fail — the simplest way to convert range reps into course pressure.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Practical Golf's Ultimate Wedge Drill is built on a behavioral fact: golfers practice the same distance in blocks (ten at 80, ten at 70), but on the course they never get the same shot twice in a row. Block practice builds mechanics, not decision-making or distance-switching feel. Random practice — a different distance every shot — builds course-ready execution that block work never reaches. The "5 questions" structure adds pass/fail accountability: five targets across your wedge range, one shot each with a full routine, 1 point per shot inside your window. Five distances, one shot each, real pressure — a far better simulation of the five or six wedge shots you face in a round than fifty blocks of one distance. **Who it helps most.** Casual, developing, and competitive players who own their stock carries but struggle to execute under course conditions — the "practice well, play poorly" wedge player is almost always over-doing block practice. Beginners benefit from the routine element with adjusted targets. **Setup.** One or two scoring wedges; an open bay with visual markers or GPS; a scoring card. Decide the target window first: 10 yards for casual/developing, 6 for competitive. **How.** Each shot: (1) call/write a specific, randomly chosen distance (e.g., 47, 73, 88, 62, 55 yards); (2) run a full pre-shot routine; (3) hit; (4) score whether it landed in the window. Five shots, five scores; rest 60 seconds; run again with five new distances. Four to six tests per session. 3+/5 is passing for developing players; log session scores — improvement here predicts on-course wedge improvement better than any block metric. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes (4–6 tests, 20–30 shots); run it as the closing segment of any wedge session — block practice to warm up mechanics, random test to convert to skill. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 2–3 of 5 within a 12-yard window; routine on all 5. • Developing: 3–4 of 5 within 10 yards; routine holds under pressure. • Competitive: 4–5 of 5 within 6 yards; logs and tracks weekly. **Common mistakes.** Choosing comfortable distances instead of truly random ones (must include the uncomfortable half- and three-quarter ranges); skipping the routine because it feels slow; doing the test cold with no warm-up and using a bad score as "proof" wedge practice doesn't work; counting shots that roll to the target rather than carry.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,dispersion}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Practical Golf$drill$, 'https://practical-golf.com/the-ultimate-wedge-practice-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Towel Connection Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$Tuck a towel under both armpits before you chip to instantly feel — and fix — the arms-from-body disconnect that causes fat and thin misses.$drill$,
 5, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** When the upper arms drift from the torso in the takeaway or downswing, club path becomes unreliable and the low point shifts. The towel gives binary, instant feedback: arms connected, towel stays; arms flying, towel drops. There's no way to cheat or misread it, which is why it works even as a five-minute pre-round warm-up. It trains body-driven chipping rather than hand-driven chipping — the foundation every sound short game is built on. **Who it helps most.** All levels, but beginners and casual players gain the most because arm disconnection is the single most common cause of the chunks and thin blades that plague new golfers around the green. Developing and competitive players use it as a start-of-session calibration to restore timing after a layoff or confirm feel before competition. **Setup.** Fold a medium towel lengthwise; tuck one end under each upper arm so the towel lies across your chest. Normal chip address — ball center, slight weight forward, hands ahead. No tee or target line needed. **How.** Make small chip swings — no longer than hip-height back and through — keeping both upper arms lightly against your sides so the towel stays. Dropping on the backswing = lifting the arms early; dropping at impact = scooping/flipping. 10 small swings without a ball to feel connection, then 10 actual chips to a spot; reset and repeat for 2–3 sets. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; the first thing at every short-game session and a pre-round green warm-up. 2–3×/week ingrains it; daily during a swing change is fine. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: towel stays on 7 of 10 hip-height swings. • Developing: holds through 10 consecutive; both arms engaged at follow-through. • Competitive: a pre-round check only — 10 consecutive holds without conscious thought. **Common mistakes.** Tucking the towel too loosely so it falls on a correct swing (snug, not squeezing); swinging too big (this is for chip-length motions); clamping the arms so tight that shoulder rotation is blocked — the goal is light connection, not a grip.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Connection Towel Drill (mygolfinstructor.com)$drill$, 'https://mygolfinstructor.com/instruction/diagnosing-problems/drills/connection-towel-drill/80/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Anti-Flip Chip$drill$,
 $drill$Combine Lorena Ochoa's trail-hand-only chip with Jon Hearn's alignment-stick-to-ribcage drill to eliminate the wrist flip behind chunked and skulled chips.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The flip — the trail wrist collapsing and the hands scooping through impact — causes the majority of inconsistent chips. These two drills attack the same fault from complementary angles. Ochoa's trail-hand-only chip forces the right wrist to keep its angle because there's no lead hand to mask a collapse — hold the angle and make clean contact, or the ball goes nowhere. Hearn's stick drill then reinforces it with both hands: the stick on your ribcage must not jam your side at impact, which only happens if the body drives the motion rather than the hands flipping. Together they encode a descending, body-led strike. **Who it helps most.** Anyone who chunks or thins chips — the flip is nearly universal in higher handicaps. Developing and competitive players use the Hearn stick drill as a targeted fix, especially if they add speed through the hands under pressure. **Setup.** Phase 1 (Ochoa): ball center, weight slightly left, only the trail hand on the grip, lead arm hanging. Phase 2 (Hearn): normal grip; hold an alignment stick or spare wedge so it points up and rests lightly on the inside of the trail ribcage; front foot slightly open, 70–80% weight forward. (Hearn teaches three ball positions — back armpit for spin, sternum for check, front armpit for release — start at sternum.) **How.** Phase 1: 15 trail-hand chips to a spot ~20 feet away, keeping the right wrist cupped (not collapsed) through impact; clean low contact = angle held; skull/chunk = flip. Phase 2: hip-height swings with the stick — if it jams the ribcage, the body stalled and the hands took over; reset. 10 with the stick, 10 without, alternating until body-drive feels natural. ~15 minutes. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; the Ochoa phase is also a 5-minute pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 10 of 15 trail-hand chips make clean, low contact and travel the intended distance. • Developing: stick drill 10 consecutive without ribcage contact; ball-first on 9 of 10. • Competitive: both phases require no conscious thought — a check when under-pressure flip creeps back. **Common mistakes.** Holding the stick too rigidly (restricts shoulder turn — it rests, not locks); in the Ochoa drill, letting the lead arm push the club through instead of staying out of the way; picking a landing spot too far away, which masks a flip (ball still travels but contact is poor).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Lorena Ochoa (Golf Digest); Jon Hearn (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/news/simple-drill-justin-rose-short-game-coach-fixed-chipping-woes/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Harrington Weight-Forward Chip$drill$,
 $drill$Stake an alignment stick just inside your front heel so you can never shift your weight back through impact — Harrington's cure for chunked chips.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The most common cause of chunked chips is what Harrington identifies as moving your center of gravity backward in the downswing — the body instinctively trying to help the ball up. The club then bottoms out behind the ball. A stick in the ground just inside the front heel creates a consequence for that shift: touch it and you know you moved back. The immediate, undeniable feedback corrects the pattern faster than any swing thought, and it builds the "sitting on the left side" sensation Harrington calls the foundation of ball-first contact. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who chunk chips, especially those who feel the urge to help the ball up or were taught to keep weight even. Players returning from a break recalibrate forward-weight patterns quickly with it. **Setup.** On a chipping area, plant an alignment stick vertically (3–4 inches up) just inside the front heel — close enough to touch your leg if your weight moves back, but not touching at address. Normal chip address, ball slightly behind center, hands ahead. **How.** Chip with the sole goal of never touching the stick. Harrington's cue: "sit" on the lead side — lead hip quiet, weight pressing into the front foot throughout, hands ahead of the ball at impact, front side loaded (not rising). 10 chips per set, reset, 3 sets. At home: front heel on carpet, take practice divots on the carpet — same pattern, no stick. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2–3×/week; especially effective in the first 10 minutes of a short-game session before target practice. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: completes a 10-chip set with no stick contact on at least 7 swings; contact noticeably cleaner. • Developing: 10 consecutive with no contact; ball-first on all 10. **Common mistakes.** Setting the stick so far from the foot it never gets touched (must actually challenge); over-leaning so far left the spine angle changes and contact goes thin; watching the stick instead of the ball at impact.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Padraig Harrington (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/drill-padraig-harrington-consistent-chipping/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Hinge & Hold$drill$,
 $drill$Break the wrists sharply going back and hold the angle through impact — Phil Mickelson's method for consistent, spin-controlling chips.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Mickelson argues in Secrets of the Short Game that hinge-and-hold is the fundamentally sound chipping technique because it produces a descending blow, creates backspin, and allows precise speed control. The "hinge" — an immediate wrist break going back — sets a steep angle that ensures a downward path and ball-before-turf contact. The "hold" — keeping that wrist angle through and past impact — prevents the scoop. Mickelson is explicit: be aggressive into and through the ball. The held angle plus a short, accelerating follow-through is what makes the ball check rather than run out, giving control on tight pins and firm greens. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who already control ball-first contact and want to add trajectory and spin control. Not a beginner drill — the steep angle causes mis-hits without a contact foundation. **Setup.** A 60° lob wedge from just off the green (Mickelson's most-shown lie). Ball back of center, 60–70% weight forward, hands pressed ahead — a compact address, not reaching. **How.** Take the club back with an immediate, deliberate hinge (clubhead near vertical by the time hands are waist height) — the hinge begins the moment the club moves, not late. Then drive the body aggressively through while holding the wrist angle; the follow-through is short and low, exiting left rather than swinging up. Drill: 5 chips to a spot, confirm the ball checks, move the target back 5 feet, repeat. 3 sets of 5 per distance, 3 distances. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; also the first few chips of any short-game session to activate the hinge feel. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean contact and a noticeable check at landing on 6 of 10. • Competitive: 9 of 10 land within 3 feet of the intended landing zone and check to within 5 feet of the hole from 20 yards. **Common mistakes.** Hinging late (one-piece takeaway then a flick — loses the steep angle); holding the angle but decelerating (the hold only works if you accelerate; deceleration = fat); using it on 40-yard pitches (most effective on greenside chips).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,contact,trajectory}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Phil Mickelson, Secrets of the Short Game (video)$drill$, null, 'oga', true),

($drill$Towel Landing-Zone Pitch$drill$,
 $drill$Land pitches on towels at 5, 10, and 15 feet onto the green to train the backswing-length distance control behind Lydia Ko's remarkably consistent pitching.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Most golfers control pitch distance by varying swing speed — faster for longer, slower for shorter. Lydia Ko's technique is the opposite: a consistent tempo across all distances, adjusting the *length* of the backswing instead. PGA.com notes Ko keeps "a remarkably consistent tempo in her pitching motion," with the cue being "changing the length of your backswing rather than altering your swing speed." Towel targets make it tangible: instead of thinking about distance to the hole (which invites speed manipulation), you aim at a specific landing zone and commit to landing on it. Over time this calibrates backswing arc to carry distance — a far more repeatable system than regulating clubhead speed. **Who it helps most.** Casual players with inconsistent pitch distances; developing players moving from speed-control to arc-control; competitive players sharpening carry precision on approach pitches where the landing zone matters more than total distance. **Setup.** Three small hand towels on the green at 5, 10, and 15 feet from the edge; stand ~25–35 yards out; a comfortable 50°–56° scoring wedge. The towels are the targets — no other aids. **How.** Land all four of a set on the first towel (5 feet on), aiming only at the landing zone — not the hole, not total distance. Then 4 balls at the second towel (10 feet on), then the third (12 balls = one rotation). Commit to backswing-length discipline: pick the arc, keep tempo constant regardless of towel distance. Track balls landing on or within a club-length of the target. 2–3 rotations per session. **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes, 2–3×/week; transfers directly — before a course pitch, visualize a landing zone, not the flag. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 12 land within a club-length of the intended towel. • Developing: 9 of 12 within a foot. • Competitive: 3 of 4 per towel land on the towel across all three distances; carry repeatable within 2 feet. **Common mistakes.** Changing tempo between towels instead of only backswing length (the whole point is tempo discipline); towels too close together (no differentiation); reverting to targeting the hole once the ball's in the air.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,trajectory}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$PGA.com (Lydia Ko-inspired)$drill$, 'https://pga.com/story/this-chipping-drill-inspired-by-lydia-ko-will-improve-your-short-game', 'oga', true),

($drill$2-Ball Bounce Pitch$drill$,
 $drill$Set two balls in your stance and pitch through both to train the flat, skimming bounce-contact that replaces steep, choppy wedge strikes.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game,range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Brech Spradley (GOLF Top 100 Teacher) designed this to solve the most common pitching fault: approaching too steeply, which drives the leading edge into the turf for chunked, hosel-heavy, or armsy pitches. The two-ball setup — one ball slightly forward of center, one near the front foot — forces a shallow, sweeping arc. To contact both with one swing the club must skim along the turf rather than stab down. Spradley emphasizes keeping "a longer flat spot" and feeling "the bounce skimming the grass" rather than digging — like landing a plane along the ground, not arriving steeply. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who strike longer clubs well but get steep or choppy with short wedges inside 40 yards; players who struggle with soft-landing finesse pitches where bounce — not leading-edge contact — is the mechanism. **Setup.** On a mat, turf, or grassy area: one ball just forward of center, a second near the front foot, ~6–8 inches apart along the target line. Normal pitching stance with a 56°–60° wedge, slight forward weight, handle just ahead of the lead ball. **How.** Make a normal pitch swing, contacting both balls in one continuous swing; the arc that catches both feels shallow and sweeping. Clip the back ball then brush the front = correct. Only the front = flipping. Top or miss both = lifting. 10 two-ball sets, then remove the front ball and hit 10 single-ball pitches with the same flat-arc feel; alternate, 3 sets total. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; effective on a range mat if no chipping green is accessible. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: both balls contacted cleanly on 7 of 10 sets; single-ball pitches noticeably softer-landing. • Competitive: 9 of 10 sets; finesse pitches land and check within 4 feet on a typical firm surface. **Common mistakes.** Balls too close together (no real arc change required); too much forward shaft lean (goal is shallow, not delofted); abandoning the two-ball setup before the flat-arc feel transfers to the single ball.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,contact,trajectory}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Brech Spradley, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/2-ball-pitching-drill-brech-spradley/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Trajectory Club Rotation$drill$,
 $drill$Chip to one target with a lob wedge, pitching wedge, and 7-iron in rotation to learn how loft controls flight-to-roll and build a three-club short game.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Distillery's framework rests on understanding that club loft — not swing speed or setup change — is the primary driver of flight-to-roll ratio around the green. A lob wedge (60°) carries ~70–80% of total distance and rolls 20–30%; a pitching wedge is roughly 50/50; a 7-iron flies ~20% and rolls ~80%. Hitting one target with all three using the same setup and chipping action (adjusting only backswing length) teaches you to match club selection to the specific carry-obstacle-vs-available-roll a greenside situation presents. It's a decision-making and feel exercise as much as technique. *(Flight-to-roll percentages are rules of thumb; they vary with green firmness and lie.)* **Who it helps most.** Beginners and casual players who default to one club (usually a wedge) for every chip; developing players who know the principle but haven't drilled the club-selection decision enough to make it automatic on course. **Setup.** One target ~20 feet from the chipping area; a lob (58°–60°), a pitching wedge (44°–46°), and a 7-iron. Standard chip address for all three — weight slightly forward, ball center, hands ahead. The setup does not change between clubs — only backswing length, to account for each loft's distance. **How.** Chip 3 balls to the target with the lob wedge, watching land + roll; 3 with the pitching wedge (lower flight, more roll); 3 with the 7-iron (minimal flight, long run-out). Score 1 point per ball finishing within 3 feet (5 feet for beginners). Two rotations per set; track your most and least accurate club. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 2–3×/week; play it against a friend for score to sharpen club-selection decisions faster. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner: 5 of 9 (one rotation, all clubs) within 5 feet. • Casual: 6 of 9 within 4 feet, aware of how each club behaves. • Developing: 7 of 9 within 3 feet; can name the right club for a given situation before chipping. **Common mistakes.** Changing setup between clubs (the point is one motion, different loft); swinging the 7-iron too hard because it feels odd for chipping (backswing just shortens, tempo constant); not watching trajectory closely enough to internalize the visual differences.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,distance_control,chip_contact}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Golf Distillery$drill$, 'https://golfdistillery.com/golf-tips/short-game/chipping/distance-control/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Bunker Sand-Entry$drill$,
 $drill$Draw a line in the sand and drop a dollar bill on it to train the precise sand-entry point — 1–2 inches behind the ball — that every consistent bunker shot needs.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The two common bunker failures — skulls over the green and chunks that stay in the sand — both trace to an inconsistent sand-entry point. GOLFTEC's Line Exercise establishes a visual reference: a line drawn perpendicular to the target, one to two inches behind the ball, with the goal of equal sand displaced on both sides after impact (a centered, consistent entry). Brech Spradley's dollar-bill variation adds tactile feedback without a ball: a bill in the sand flutters out toward the target if the bounce and entry point are right, but stays buried or shoots sideways if the leading edge digs or the entry is too far back. Visual plus physical confirmation of proper technique. **Who it helps most.** All levels — bunker play is undertrained at every handicap. Beginners learn the foundational entry point; casual/developing players recalibrate a fat- or thin-entry habit; competitive players run the line drill before competition to confirm bounce and entry on that day's sand. **Setup.** Open the sand-wedge face slightly (pointing a touch skyward); open the stance left of target (righty); draw a line across the target line; place a dollar bill (or folded paper) centered on it, pressed lightly into the sand, with a ball 1–2 inches in front. For the GOLFTEC no-ball version, omit the ball first. **How.** Phase 1 (no ball): 5 practice swings bottoming out exactly on the line — not behind, not ahead — checking for equal sand on both sides. Phase 2 (bill + ball): swing with the same entry focus; the bill should flutter out toward the target as the ball pops out soft. Bill buried = too steep or too far back. 10 shots with the bill, 10 without, carrying the feel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week; a 5-minute pre-round warm-up whenever a practice bunker's available. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner: 6 of 10 bill shots flutter the bill toward target; ball exits consistently. • Casual: 7 of 10; feeling how sand conditions change required speed. • Developing: 9 of 10; can adjust entry and speed for firm vs soft sand. • Competitive: all 10; line drill used only to calibrate unusual sand. **Common mistakes.** Drawing the line too far behind the ball (heavy, short shots); too little face-open so the leading edge leads instead of the bounce (the face should feel almost uncomfortably open); decelerating — you must accelerate, as sand absorbs speed.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker}', 'blocked',
 $drill$GOLFTEC; Brech Spradley (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/bunker-shots/100-bill-bunker-drill-brech-spradley/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Bunker No-Ball Splash$drill$,
 $drill$Make 10 aggressive splash swings without a ball to build sand-contact feel and commitment tempo before your first real bunker shot.$drill$,
 5, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Bunker shots fail more from hesitation than mechanics. The distinctive feel of the club skimming through sand — the resistance, the forward explosion, the high follow-through — is hard to learn for the first time under pressure. No-ball splash swings remove the anxiety of a result and let the body repeat the correct club-through-sand sensation quickly. PGA teaching stresses practicing "until it feels natural," because a bunker shot is fundamentally different from every other shot: you're hitting the sand under the ball, not the ball. Without a ball to fixate on, that intent gets clearer and the swing more committed. **Who it helps most.** All levels equally. Beginners learn the motion safely without a defiant ball sitting in the sand; casual/developing players restore feel after time away; competitive players use it as a mandatory pre-round warm-up — the first 10 swings in any bunker should be no-ball to read how the sand plays that day. **Setup.** Open the sand- or lob-wedge face (slightly skyward); open the stance; twist your feet a half-inch in to gauge depth; address a patch of sand as if a ball sat there; draw a small entry line 1–2 inches behind the imaginary ball. **How.** Make 10 full, committed splash swings — not tentative pokes. Enter at the line and continue through, the bounce skimming forward; sand should spray toward the target. A tidy forward explosion = correct entry and depth. If the club catches and stops, you led with the edge and decelerated — reset with more speed and a more open face. After 10, assess: soft/fluffy sand (slower, less sand) or firm (more speed, shallower entry). **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes, always the first activity in any bunker session; on the course, 5–8 no-ball splashes before your first ball is the highest-value bunker warm-up available. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner: 7 of 10 produce a forward-moving sand explosion. • Casual: 8 of 10 consistent in shape and depth. • Developing: 10 of 10 controlled splashes; a verbal read on the sand before the first ball. • Competitive: automatic; dialing exact sand speed for the day. **Common mistakes.** Tentative half-speed swings that bury the club (commit and accelerate); entering too far behind the imaginary ball (too-heavy splash); not opening the face enough so the edge catches and the club stops.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$PGA.com$drill$, 'https://pga.com/story/how-to-hit-any-bunker-shot', 'oga', true),

($drill$Up-and-Down Scoring Game$drill$,
 $drill$Play a par-18 chipping course — nine stations, par 2 each — to turn short-game practice into scored competition that mirrors real up-and-down pressure.$drill$,
 25, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Practical Golf, GOLFTEC, and MyGolfSpy converge on one principle: unstructured chipping (buckets to no consequence) builds volume, not pressure-tested skill. The par-18 format — nine stations, each a par-2 up-and-down — replicates the decision-making and consequence of a real hole: commit to a landing zone, execute under a score, and putt out every shot. Practical Golf's version has you complete each hole (chip up + putt out) in as few strokes as possible. Over time your baseline par-18 score becomes a measurable benchmark, giving objective data on short-game improvement a bucket of chips never can. **Who it helps most.** Casual players who want practice that doesn't feel like a chore; developing players who have technique but fail to execute on course (the range-to-round gap); competitive players simulating pressure and tracking up-and-down percentage. **Setup.** Nine stations around the practice green at different angles, lies, and distances — from 5 yards off to 30, tight lies to rough, uphill to downhill. With a partner, alternate who places stations so neither can game them; you can also assign each station's lie (sitting up/down/fluffy) for variety. Each station is a par-2 hole. **How.** Start at Station 1: chip and putt out, counting every stroke; record the hole score. Move through all 9; total against par 18. Track session over session — 18 means up-and-down everywhere; 24 means 2.67/hole, a number to beat. Competitive players set a stations-par target (par-18 scratch, par-22 for a 15-handicap). Scale difficulty: beginners use only greenside stations within 10 yards; competitive players add 25–30-yard semi-rough lies. **Time & frequency.** 20–30 minutes, 2–3×/week; shorten to 6 holes (par 12) for 15-minute sessions. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 9 holes in ≤27 (≈3/hole, up-and-down ~half). • Developing: 18–22 (up-and-down on 6–7 of 9). • Competitive: 18–19 (near-perfect rate from varied lies). **Common mistakes.** Cheating with only easy, flat lies (must include awkward angles/distances to transfer); not putting out every hole (defeats the up-and-down simulation); skipping score-tracking (removes the feedback loop that makes the format work).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$GOLFTEC; Practical Golf; MyGolfSpy$drill$, 'https://practical-golf.com/chipping-drills/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Pre-Round Putting Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$A structured three-station warm-up that primes lag speed, break reads, and short-putt confidence before your first tee shot.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Cold putting is the most predictable way to three-putt the first hole. Green speed varies course to course and day to day with moisture and mow height, so a stroke calibrated to your home green is wrong everywhere else. GOLF Top 100 Teacher Michael Hunt splits the warm-up into three purposeful stations — distance feel, break reading, and the confidence of watching putts drop. Ten minutes primes the visual-motor loop before you've hit a tee shot, so your stroke isn't running a deficit for the first three holes. **Who it helps most.** Every level, but high-handicappers who rush to the range and skip the green gain the most — they reach the first green cold to speed and line. Competitive players use it as final calibration, not discovery. **Setup.** Putter and a handful of balls. A green with (1) a long flat area for lag, (2) a 12–15-foot putt with visible break, (3) a straight 3–5-foot putt. **How.** Station 1 (lag): 3–6 balls from the longest putt, focus on speed only — no target hole; lengthen the stroke and calibrate to today's pace. Station 2 (break read): from 12–15 feet, place a tee at your read's apex and roll the ball at the tee, confirming the read and that pace reaches the apex; 4–5 attempts. Station 3 (short): on a straight 3-footer (low handicaps step to 4–5 feet), set a club on the target line and roll putts until you've watched 5–6 drop — like a free-throw shooter finding rhythm; end on a make. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes before every round; a pre-round protocol, not a standalone session. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: complete all three stations without rushing; end on a made short putt. • Developing: station-2 read on the correct side on 3 of 5; short putts 100% from 3 feet. • Competitive: read accurate AND pace within a foot of the tee on 4 of 5; a clean run of 5 from 4–5 feet before leaving. **Common mistakes.** Spending all 10 minutes holing 3-footers (lag is where rounds bleed strokes); rushing station 1 with three lazy lag putts; using a featureless lie for station 2 that gives no read feedback.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{lag,short_putts,green_reading}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Michael Hunt, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/three-drills-dial-in-putting-weekend-warmup/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Gate Drill$drill$,
 $drill$Roll putts through a narrow tee gate to groove a square face and a pure start line.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** On a putt, face angle at impact controls the large majority of the ball's starting direction — putting-launch research commonly cites ~83%, up to ~90% on a center strike — while path barely matters at short range. Most missed short putts aren't misreads; they're tiny face errors, a degree or two, invisible at address. The gate makes that error unmissable: an off-square face clips a tee. Binary feedback on the single thing that matters most inside 10 feet, a staple from David Leadbetter to tour coaches. **Who it helps most.** Every level benefits, but developing and competitive players already rolling a reasonable stroke get the most — the gate exposes the last 1–2°. Beginners use a slightly wider gate as a habit-builder. **Setup.** Flat, straight 5–10 ft putt; two tees just outside toe and heel, a few mm clearance each side (wider for higher handicaps); ball midway; optional second gate ahead to check start line. **How.** Normal stroke, roll through; clean pass = square face + center strike; a clipped tee shows the miss direction; sets of ten, reset the count on any miss. **Time & frequency.** 5–10 min, 2–3×/week; also a sharp pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 6–7 of 10 clean at 5 ft. • Developing: 8–9 of 10 at 6–8 ft. • Competitive: 10 in a row, then back up to 10 ft. **Common mistakes.** A gate so wide it never gives feedback; steering the stroke to dodge the tees; staring at the gate instead of the hole.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$David Leadbetter / Titleist / HackMotion$drill$, 'https://hackmotion.com/gate-putting-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Start-Line Track$drill$,
 $drill$Roll putts down a string, chalk line, or ruler board to prove the ball launches square off the face before any break is possible.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The ball's initial direction is decided in roughly the first 6–12 inches of roll — after that, slope and gravity take over. If the face is even 2° off, no amount of green-reading accuracy can save the putt. Three players use three physical versions of the same idea: Jessica Korda stretches a string just above a straight 5–10-foot putt so a mis-started roll wobbles off it immediately and the string anchors eye and face alignment; Kellie Stenzel snaps a chalk line on a straight section — no setup, zero ambiguity about where the ball actually rolled; Lydia Ko rolls putts down a narrow ruler/board — an open face drops the ball off the right edge early, closed off the left. All three measure the same thing: does the ball track straight off the face? **Who it helps most.** Any player with a repeating one-sided miss — that pattern almost always traces to face angle, not the read. Casual through competitive; beginners build stroke fundamentals with the Gate Drill first. **Setup.** A dead-straight 6–10-foot putt; choose a tool — a string tied between two tees elevated an inch above the path, a chalk snap line, or a ruler/board with the ball at one end — aligned exactly on the start line. **How.** String/chalk: normal stroke, observe whether the ball tracks the line its full rolling distance before breaking. Board: roll down its length — any wobble or early departure reveals face direction. Sets of 10, noting which side departures occur. After 10 clean in a row, add a gentle breaking putt to confirm the ball still starts on line before the curve. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2–3×/week during inconsistent stretches; the chalk version is a fast pre-round check if the green allows. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 7 of 10 rolling the full intended line at 6 feet. • Developing: 9 of 10 tracking clean at 8 feet. • Competitive: 10 of 10 at 8–10 feet, then reproduce the start line on 10 free putts without the aid. **Common mistakes.** Setting up on a putt with hidden micro-break (find genuinely flat ground); speeding up the stroke to "help" the ball stay on line; on the board, gripping tighter to control the face (kills the feel feedback).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Jessica Korda (Golf Digest); Kellie Stenzel (Golf.com); Lydia Ko (PGA.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/10-favorite-putting-drills-kellie-stenzel/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Distance Ladder$drill$,
 $drill$Putt through a graduated ladder of distances with a scoring system that rewards smart pace, not just made putts.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Three-putts rarely happen because you can't make a 30-footer; they happen because the first putt finished 8 feet past or 6 short. Distance ladders train the skill that prevents them: controlling speed across a range of distances under mild scoring pressure. Three sources give the same progressive architecture with useful variations — the Golf.com ladder uses birdie/par/bogey scoring for urgency; Tessa Teachman's Leapfrog (SuperStroke) pushes each ball just past the previous for pure increment feel; PGA Tour player Sam Ryder uses two coins as a finish zone across five distances, restarting on any miss to build commitment to pace over direction. **Who it helps most.** Casual through developing players who three-putt from 20–40 feet (the scoring pressure is the value); competitive players use the restart-on-miss variant as a tournament-stakes simulation. **Setup.** Golf.com format: a tee one pace (~3 feet) behind the hole; balls at 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 feet. Ryder format: two coins 2.5 feet apart centered on the hole; tees at 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 feet. Leapfrog: a start tee and a 15-foot boundary; no hole. **How.** Golf.com: roll the five in distance order — holed = birdie (+1), finishing between cup and tee = par (0), short or hitting the tee = bogey (−1); goal 1-under across five (step each ball back two paces to push the longest to ~30 feet for more difficulty). Ryder: from 15 feet, stop a ball between the coins; advance through 20–35; any putt outside the coins resets to 15. Leapfrog: each ball lands past the previous one until one lands short; beat your streak. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; pair one Ryder run (precision under pressure) with one Leapfrog run (incremental feel). **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: even or better in the Golf.com format at 3–15 feet; reach ball 4 in Leapfrog without backtracking. • Developing: 1-under in Golf.com; complete the Ryder ladder 15–35 with no restart. • Competitive: 2-under on the extended ladder; complete the Ryder ladder twice without a reset. **Common mistakes.** Treating it as make-or-miss (the finish zone, not the hole, is the long-putt target — a 2-foot-past lip-out is a failure); only using downhill putts (skews feel firm; mix slopes so uphill pace develops too).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Golf.com; Tessa Teachman/SuperStroke; Sam Ryder (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/use-ladder-drill-roll-putts-like-pga-tour-pro/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tee-Corridor Lag (Never Short)$drill$,
 $drill$Build a safe-zone corridor behind the hole so every lag finishes past the cup but within tap-in range, eliminating the costliest miss in putting.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dave Pelz spent decades collecting data on where tour lag putts actually finish; the finding is unambiguous: the most common and costly lag error is finishing short, which is by definition a zero-percent outcome. The corridor drill makes "never short" physical — a back boundary forces you to program a ball finishing 1–3 feet past rather than short. From 40–60 feet, Pelz's "good zone" (sometimes called the safe-zone) is the target: optimal lag speed finishes about 17 inches past the hole, and the zone extends as a semicircle behind the cup out to roughly 34 inches past — so a lag that dies 1–3 feet past is a success, and short is the one outcome to eliminate. The drill's deeper message is directional: when in doubt, be past. **Who it helps most.** Casual through developing players who chronically leave long putts short — the "never up, never in" fear response is deeply ingrained in amateurs. Competitive players sharpen the 40–60-foot range where lag strokes-gained separates fields. **Setup.** Mark 40, 50, 60 feet from the cup. Build the corridor: a tee 17 inches short of the hole and a tee 34 inches past; the safe zone is between them, with preference for the far (past-center) half. **How.** Start at 50 feet: roll three balls aiming to finish inside the corridor, favoring the past-center half. Advance to 40 on three corridor hits, then 60 on three more; the 10th putt is a single pressure putt from 50. Track the sequence and try to beat your record; Pelz's benchmark is 10 corridor hits to complete the run. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; extend during any stretch of long-putt three-putts. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 10 inside the corridor from 50 feet. • Developing: complete 40/50/60 with ≤2 corridor misses total. • Competitive: the full 10-putt progression without a miss, from both flat and 2% slope. **Common mistakes.** A corridor so wide it gives no feedback (far tee at most ~34–36 inches past); fixating on the back edge so pace drifts long and the comeback becomes a second test; flat-surface-only practice (your speed calibration is then wrong on every real hole).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Dave Pelz (RxGolfNetwork / Putting Bible)$drill$, 'https://rxgolfnetwork.com/lag-putt-drill-with-dave-pelz/', 'oga', true),

($drill$3-Foot Circle$drill$,
 $drill$Hole 12 balls in a clock-face circle at 3 feet — including breaking putts from every point — and restart on any miss.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Pelz's research showed PGA Tour players make ~95% of 3-foot putts in competition while 20-handicaps make ~81% — a gap that costs real strokes across 18 holes (Pelz's in-play figures, not PGA Tour ShotLink, which lumps in tap-ins). The clock-face circle addresses why: most players practice 3-footers flat, straight, and from one direction, so a 3-footer that breaks 2 inches or runs downhill — the majority on real courses — has no ingrained reference. Twelve positions around the hole means every putt breaks a different amount: the two fall-line positions (straight up/down) are zero-break anchors, the diagonals are moderate breakers, the two side positions curl the full slope and are hardest. The restart-on-miss rule adds the pressure that makes holing a routine short putt feel familiar under competition. Phil Mickelson credits Hall-of-Famer Jackie Burke with an advanced version — 10 balls around the hole with break, make 100 in a row before leaving — using a 25-back/75-through stroke ratio for forward-rolling momentum. **Who it helps most.** Everyone. Beginners/casual players build baseline reliability and exposure to every break type; developing/competitive players use the restart rule to replicate par-saving pressure. **Setup.** Find the cup's fall line; mark 12 positions at exactly 3 feet, clock-face spaced, 12 o'clock straight uphill, 6 o'clock straight downhill; one ball each. **How.** Start at 6 o'clock and putt counter-clockwise; make all 12, one miss resets to the start; track your best streak. Advanced (Mickelson): 10 balls at 3 feet with gentle break, make 100 consecutive, with Burke's 25/75 tempo. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; a full circuit takes 3–5 minutes if you're holing them, and pressure builds near putt 10–11. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: one full circuit of 12 without a miss at least once per session (8 before a miss is a solid start). • Developing: 3 full circuits; extra reps at the side positions (3 and 9 o'clock). • Competitive: 3 circuits consistently; attempt the Mickelson 100-in-a-row (a realistic short-session target of 50 consecutive). **Common mistakes.** Practicing only the comfortable straight-uphill position while rushing the downhill/side positions (the ones that cost strokes); starting too far back (nail 3 feet first); on the Mickelson version, drifting the stroke longer on both ends instead of 25/75 (a decelerating stroke, left-side misses).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dave Pelz (Putting Bible); progression: Phil Mickelson (Secrets of the Short Game)$drill$, 'https://synlawngolf.com/partnerships/dave-pelz/golf-practice/elements-of-practice-short-putts/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Confidence-Builder (Yarwood Hole-Feel)$drill$,
 $drill$Make the same 4-foot putt five different ways to rewire your brain into seeing the hole as bigger than it feels.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The 4.25-inch cup fits more than two golf balls side by side. Most amateurs who flinch on short putts don't flinch because the hole is small — they've mentally shrunk it to a keyhole by fixating on one "correct" entry. GOLF Top 100 Teacher Jonathan Yarwood's drill is a psychological reset: repeated makes with varied entry points prove the cup accepts a wide range of speeds and angles. Yarwood describes it as "psychologically allowing you to understand that ball goes in a lot of ways" — once you've watched it fall through the left edge, the right edge, the back, and at dying pace in one session, the association shifts and the hole looks bigger. Perceived target size genuinely correlates with confidence and performance under pressure. **Who it helps most.** Beginners and casual players building a foundation; developing players with a sound stroke missing short putts under pressure from tightening. Competitive players doing full pressure training get more from the 3-Foot Circle — this drill is about confidence repair and perceptual expansion. **Setup.** One cup on a flat or mildly sloped surface at ~4 feet; at least 5 balls; no alignment aids — this is feel and intention, not mechanical tracking. **How.** Roll five putts targeting five entry types: (1) center, perfect pace; (2) center, dying pace, toppling in on the last revolution; (3) center, firm into the back of the cup; (4) right edge — aim at the right side and fall in off the right lip; (5) left edge. Each made putt reinforces a pathway. Miss one, don't reset — repeat that entry until holed, then continue. Cover all five, then repeat the sequence 2–3 times. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week during short-putt yips or confidence loss; a good pre-round alternative to high-pressure gate work. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: complete all five entry types at least once per session. • Developing: two consecutive full sequences (10 putts, all five types twice) without missing any attempt. • Competitive: three quick sequences as a reset; move to the Circle Drill for pressure reps. **Common mistakes.** Cheating the "dying speed" entry by hitting it firmly (the point is the slowest possible make); using a gate/aid (turns a psychological exercise into a mechanical one); treating a miss as a crisis instead of noting the entry type and repeating until made.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{short_putts}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Jonathan Yarwood, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/tour-proven-drill-improve-putting-play-smart/', 'oga', true),

($drill$One-Handed Stroke$drill$,
 $drill$Putt trail-hand-only then lead-hand-only to develop the independent feel that creates topspin, tempo, and a shorter stroke.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods uses trail-hand-only putting as a pre-round warm-up, and GOLF Top 100 Teacher Scott Munroe explains the mechanics: the trail hand (right, for righties) is the primary feel and speed hand in most strokes. Isolating it lets the putter toe release naturally through impact — important for toe-hang designs — generating true topspin rather than a skid. Topspin produces predictable roll; skid produces unpredictable early bounce, which is why 20-footers run out differently than expected. Munroe's insight: "Speed control comes from good spin, and good spin allows you to have a shorter stroke — a shorter stroke is always better, as less can go wrong." One-handed practice makes a sloppy two-handed stroke physically impossible. The lead-hand-only version builds the resistance half — the pull that keeps the through-stroke from accelerating uncontrollably. **Who it helps most.** Developing players with yippy or jerky tempo; competitive players dialing touch and topspin before a round. Casual players can use it but may find the trail-hand version frustrating at first — start at 3–4 feet before lag distances. **Setup.** A flat green with 10–15 feet of space; your gamer putter (the toe-release benefit is most pronounced with toe-hang, but any putter works). **How.** Trail-hand only: trail hand mid-grip, lead hand behind your back; start at 3–5 feet feeling the toe release past the heel; work back to 8–10 feet; 10 putts. Lead-hand only: lead hand only, trail behind the back — this half is control, the lead hand pulling without overpowering; 10 putts from 6–10 feet. Finish two-handed: the stroke should feel smoother and lower-effort. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2–3×/week; a great pre-round touch warm-up. Keep it short — fatigue defeats a feel drill. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 10 trail-hand putts from 4 feet without visible skid. • Developing: 8 of 10 trail-hand from 6–8 feet with consistent toe-release; 7 of 10 lead-hand from the same. • Competitive: a 10-putt trail-hand run from 10–12 feet, then reproduce the pace two-handed with no speed loss. **Common mistakes.** Gripping tightly with the single hand (kills feel — let it swing); attempting lag putts before establishing short-distance feel; skipping the two-handed integration putts (negates the transfer).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Tiger Woods / Scott Munroe, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/tiger-woods-one-handed-putt-practice-strategy/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Eyes-Closed Speed Calibration$drill$,
 $drill$Stroke a putt with eyes closed, call your shot before you open them, and learn to trust kinesthetic feel over visual anxiety.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Three-time major champion Padraig Harrington uses this to solve a persistent problem: watching the putter head during the stroke rather than committing to the feel of the swing. When you can see the putter, your nervous system reacts to perceived mis-hits in real time — a twitch, a last-millisecond adjustment — and those micro-corrections turn a smooth stroke jerky. Eyes closed eliminates that loop: "if you can't see the putter head, you can't react to it." It also calibrates distance — hitting several 25–30-footers eyes-closed and predicting short/long builds an internal speed model for the day's pace (9-stimp greens feel different from 11). Some elite players have used the technique in actual competition, underscoring how deeply feel-over-sight underlies consistent putting. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with sound strokes that deteriorate under performance anxiety (the protocol disrupts the visual-reactive flinch); casual players who over-steer. Less useful for complete beginners who still need visual feedback for basic path and face awareness. **Setup.** Any putt 15–30 feet, flat or mildly breaking; no equipment; one ball per cycle. **How.** Step 1: roll a normal open-eyes putt from ~30 feet; note where it finishes. Step 2: a second ball, same distance — take your read and commit with eyes open, then close your eyes just before the backstroke and keep them closed through impact and 2 seconds after. Step 3: before opening, call it — solid strike? left or right? short or long? Step 4: open and compare. Prediction accuracy is the metric, not whether it went in. 5–8 cycles per session, occasionally from 10–15 feet. Pre-round: 4–6 eyes-closed putts from 20–30 feet to calibrate the day's speed (Harrington explicitly recommends this). **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week in technical phases; 5 minutes in every pre-round warm-up as a speed station. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: prediction matches reality (short/long, left/right) on 5 of 8. • Developing: 6–7 of 8; regularly senses heel/toe contact without seeing it. • Competitive: 7–8 of 8; can reproduce a target speed ("2 feet past") to within 18 inches on command. **Common mistakes.** Opening the eyes at impact because it feels wrong (keep them closed a full 2 seconds after); judging by hole-outs (a made putt with a wrong prediction teaches nothing; an accurate read on a miss teaches everything); skipping the prediction step (turns training into a novelty).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,tempo}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Padraig Harrington (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/padraig-harrington-eyes-closed-putting-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Two-Hole Back-and-Forth$drill$,
 $drill$Putt one ball back and forth between two holes 20–30 feet apart seven times without three-putting to build pressure-tested lag that transfers straight to the course.$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Kellie Stenzel's drill reproduces the most common putting scenario — a 20–30-foot lag plus a meaningful comeback — and adds a consecutive-success rule that turns casual reps into pressure reps. The setup is simple (one ball, two holes, a 20–30-foot gap); the challenge is the rule: two-putt or better seven consecutive times before stopping, any three-putt restarting the count. Alternating direction means every successful first putt creates a second putt of variable length and slope — sometimes 18 inches, sometimes 4 feet — and you must make all of them. That mirrors actual conditions far better than 10 lag putts at one hole in one direction with no stakes on the comeback. The back-and-forth also builds adaptability: after several trips you've putted the surface uphill, downhill, and across grain, a richer model than a single-direction ladder. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who lose strokes to three-putts (the rule makes every lag matter, because a good lag plus a missed 3-footer still resets); players with solid short-putt mechanics but poor lag commitment, who decelerate into long putts — the count creates just enough urgency to commit to a pace. **Setup.** Two holes 20–30 feet apart (farther once routine — Stenzel notes 18 consecutive is an advanced goal); ideally with mild undulation or break between them so it isn't the same flat line; one ball only. **How.** Putt from A toward B; make it = a 1-putt, walk over and putt back; two-putt also counts (tap in, walk to the finish, putt back); a three-putt resets the count to zero. Each two-putt-or-better exchange advances the count; reach 7 consecutive to complete the baseline. Track it out loud. Progression: 7 → 10 → 18 consecutive; vary your starting spot by a few feet to avoid memorizing one pace. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week; great in a pre-round warm-up since one 7-run takes 5–7 minutes and covers lag plus comebacks. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: a 7-consecutive run at least once per session (4 without a three-putt is a strong start). • Developing: two 7-runs per session; aim for 10 in a row. • Competitive: 10 consecutive minimum; push to 18 when the first 10 comes easily; note your three-putt triggers (long uphill lag? breaking comeback?) and return to them on purpose. **Common mistakes.** Holes too close (15-foot lags lose the sting — 20 feet is the floor); conceding comebacks longer than 2 feet (the comeback counts; giving it to yourself trains missing 3-footers in competition); stopping the moment you hit 7 on the first try (the drill is most valuable when you've had to restart — that's where the pressure lives).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{lag,distance_control,green_reading}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Kellie Stenzel, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/10-favorite-putting-drills-kellie-stenzel/', 'oga', true),

