-- pressure_game · off_tee
($drill$Narrowing Fairway Ladder$drill$,
 $drill$Set a 50-yard fairway on the range and hit three drives in a row inside it to advance — then narrow to 30 yards, then to 10 — and you can't move on until you clear the current width.$drill$,
 20, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind) builds pressure from a single rule: you can only move to the next stage after hitting three balls in a row inside the current "fairway." A miss doesn't just score badly — it costs you the streak you were building, and the tightening width turns mild discomfort into Tour-grade precision. Each stage replicates a different course reality: 50 yards is a generous fairway, 30 yards is a tight one, 10 yards is a corridor that demands committed start-line work. Because progress is gated and a single bad swing keeps you stuck, the drill manufactures the consequence-on-this-shot pressure a normal range bucket erases. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic strike, especially those whose driver "works on the range" but leaks under pressure. Not for beginners — you can spend the whole session at stage one and learn nothing. **Setup.** Two range targets (alignment sticks, flags, or terrain features) set ~50 yards apart to mark the edges of your first fairway. Driver, full bucket, a routine you actually use on the course. **How.** Run your full pre-shot routine on every ball. Hit three drives in a row inside the 50-yard fairway — if you miss any, the count resets but you stay at this width. After 3-of-3, narrow the fairway to ~30 yards and repeat. After 3-of-3 there, narrow to ~10 yards. Completing all three stages = nine consecutive fairways. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly on driver work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clear the 50-yard stage cleanly; chip away at 30. • Competitive: complete all three stages in a session. **Common mistakes.** Counting non-consecutive hits; widening the fairway when frustrated (kills the consequence); skipping the routine to rush; quitting before you've fought through a reset (the reset is where the lesson lives).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind)$drill$, 'https://golfstateofmind.com/5-pressure-practice-drills-for-golf/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Five-in-a-Row Fairway$drill$,
 $drill$Pick a fairway-width target on the range and hit five drives in a row inside it — any miss and the count goes back to zero. Pure consecutive-make pressure.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind) keeps the rule painfully simple: five consecutive fairways, and a single miss resets you to zero. The cumulative tension of a streak is exactly what builds up on the course as you walk to the next tee with a card going well — and most golfers crack under it because they never rehearse it on the range. By stripping out laddering, scoring, and time and just demanding five-in-a-row from a fixed width, the drill makes every shot feel like the one that breaks the streak. The single-miss reset is the consequence; the only way out is committing to the start line you actually want, every single rep. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players already past basic strike who freeze on important tee shots. Skip it for beginners — the streak may never start and the lesson disappears in noise. **Setup.** Two range targets ~30 yards apart marking a single fairway width (configurable: wider for developing, tighter for competitive). Driver, full bucket. **How.** Full routine on every ball. Count consecutive drives that finish inside the fairway. Miss = back to zero. Stop only when you've strung five in a row. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly when working tee-shot commitment. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: reach five-in-a-row at a 40–50-yard width. • Competitive: five-in-a-row at a 25–30-yard width. **Common mistakes.** Counting half-misses ("close enough"); cheating the width when the reset bites; rushing reps after a streak starts (commit to the routine — the streak punishes rushing); doing it cold without a warm-up (the drill is the test, not the warm-up).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind)$drill$, 'https://golfstateofmind.com/driving-range-practice-create-pressure-while-you-practice/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Fairway Hit Challenge (7-of-10)$drill$,
 $drill$Hit seven of ten drives into your imaginary fairway before you're allowed to end the session — a Tour-grade standard with no shortcuts, reset the set if you fall short.$drill$,
 25, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Eric J. MacDonald, PGA, frames the pressure exactly: "hit 7 out of 10 drives in the fairway before finishing your range session." The mechanism isn't a score you track — it's a session lock, the same kind of "you don't leave until you clear the bar" standard Tour players use. 70% fairways with a driver on the range is roughly Tour-grade, so failing the bar is the default state, and the *only* way to leave is to actually find seven. The consequence — restart the set, no shortcut — produces real, escalating pressure as you get to ball 8, 9, 10 with the count tight. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who want a Tour-honest standard for driving accuracy; players who tell themselves they "drive it well" without ever testing it under a hard threshold. Not for beginners (the bar is unreachable). **Setup.** Range, driver, a defined imaginary fairway width (most players set 30–40 yards; tighter is harder), and the discipline to not bail on the rule. **How.** Hit 10 drives, count fairways. If you don't get to 7, you don't end the session — reset and hit another 10. Keep going until you clear it. Run your full routine on every ball — the routine is part of the test. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes (longer if you don't clear it quickly), 1×/week as a driver session finisher. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clear 7/10 at a generous 40-yard width within two sets. • Competitive: clear 7/10 at a 30-yard width within one set. **Common mistakes.** Quietly widening the fairway when stuck; bailing on the lock-in rule (no consequence, no pressure); only counting fairways without running the routine; doing it tired at the end of a bucket (decide up front this is the session).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Eric J. MacDonald, PGA$drill$, 'https://www.ericjmacdonaldpga.com/post/practice-under-pressure-how-to-simulate-on-course-stress-in-training', 'oga', true),

-- pressure_game · approach
($drill$Pressure-Cooker (3-Target Sequence)$drill$,
 $drill$Pick three irons and three different targets and hit one shot per club, in order — miss any one and the whole three-shot sequence restarts from the first club.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Clickit Golf's resident PGA Pro frames the pressure exactly: "if you miss a target, you have to start the sequence over." The consequence isn't a worse score — it's losing every rep you just earned. Pair that with the source's mental hook ("imagine it is the 18th green" with a career-best on the line and run your full routine) and the drill turns a small set of approach shots into the back-nine pressure most players never rehearse. The sequence forces a club change every shot (so it's also random practice in flavor), but the consequence rule is the heart of the drill: a single missed window means you didn't really execute the previous two either. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who hit pure shots on the range but unravel when a single shot has to land. Not for beginners — the loop never closes and frustration is the only outcome. **Setup.** Range with three distinct target areas at different distances (a short-iron green, a mid-iron green, a wedge yardage), three clubs to match. Define the success window for each (treat a green-sized landing band as the bar). **How.** Choose your three clubs and three targets. Shot 1 with club A to target A; shot 2 with club B to target B; shot 3 with club C to target C. Full routine on every shot. Hit the success window on all three back-to-back to complete the drill. A miss at any shot → restart from shot 1, no exceptions. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly on approach work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete the sequence within a generous window over a session. • Competitive: complete it cleanly at tighter windows. **Common mistakes.** Loosening the success window when stuck; skipping the routine to "just hit"; picking three similar clubs (kills the random element); quitting after one full reset (the second attempt is where the pressure starts compounding).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,contact,dispersion}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Clickit Golf (PGA Pro)$drill$, 'https://clickitgolf.com/master-your-game-3-range-drills-you-can-do-in-just-20-minute/', 'oga', true),

-- pressure_game · around_green
($drill$Pelz Short Game Test$drill$,
 $drill$Take Dave Pelz's 80-shot short-game test across eight stations and score yourself against Tour and handicap thresholds — anything outside six feet earns zero, every weak shot is a wasted ball.$drill$,
 60, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dave Pelz (former NASA physicist turned short-game guru) built the canonical pass/fail short-game benchmark, and the consequence is built into the scoring: a chip outside 6 feet scores zero, between 3 and 6 feet scores 1 point, and inside 3 feet (including holed) scores 4 points. Because misses are worth nothing, every weak shot is a wasted ball — and your raw point total maps to a clear handicap-bracket bar you either clear or you don't. The 8 stations cover the full short-game test (pitches, sand, chips from fringe and rough, lob over bunker), so a single weakness gets caught. The Tour benchmarks make the test honest: you can't tell yourself you're a "good chipper" if you can't put up the number. **Who it helps most.** Every level — the thresholds self-calibrate to your handicap so the test is meaningful from beginner to scratch; especially useful for players who claim a strong short game without ever measuring it. **Setup.** A short-game area with chipping green, fringe, light rough, and a bunker; wedges and putter; 80 balls (or 10 you replay per station); a way to estimate 3-ft and 6-ft circles around the pin. **How.** Eight stations × 10 balls each: (1) 50-yd pitch, (2) 30-yd pitch, (3) 8-yd sand, (4) 15-yd long bunker blast, (5) 10-yd chip from light fringe, (6) 20-yd chip from light rough, (7) 15-yd short pitch, (8) 15-yd lob over a bunker. Score each shot: 0 outside 6 ft, 1 between 3–6 ft, 4 inside 3 ft (incl. hole-outs). Sum across all 80 shots. **Confirmed thresholds (per Pelz):** PGA Tour 120–150 · LPGA Tour 103–120 · 1–3 hcp 100–107 · 4–9 hcp 80–97 · 10–15 hcp 60–77 · 16–20 hcp 50–58 · 21–24 hcp 42–48 · 26–30 hcp 30–38 · <30 needs work. **Time & frequency.** ~60 minutes; monthly as a benchmark, not a daily drill. **Success metric, by level.** Clear the threshold band for your handicap. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the bunker stations (where the strokes hide); estimating distances generously; doing it in batches of one station for a week (the test is the full 80 in one session); comparing your raw score to a different distance protocol.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control,bunker}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dave Pelz (Pelz Golf, via Dog Leg News)$drill$, 'https://doglegnews.com/short-game-handicap-heres-the-test-to-determine-yours/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Last-Ball Chip-Out$drill$,
 $drill$Bring three (or six) balls; you must HOLE a chip before you retire that ball and move to a new location — the longer you go, the fewer chances you have left.$drill$,
 30, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Christy Longfield, PGA (GOLF Magazine Teacher to Watch), runs this drill — the same retire-on-make game Phil Mickelson uses in his own short-game practice. The pressure mechanism is consequence-by-attrition: you bring a fixed number of balls and you can only "retire" a ball by holing a chip with it. Move to a new location with the remaining balls, hole one out, retire it, move on. As balls get retired, you have fewer chances per spot until you're down to one ball and one shot at every new lie — which is exactly where pressure peaks. Longfield: "The longer you go, the harder it gets." Mickelson's version makes it even tighter (a missed 3-footer or a missed tight circle starts you over), so the lesson is consistent: misses bite. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose chips look fine but aren't actually holing out under pressure; players preparing for tournament short-game requirements. **Setup.** A short-game green with multiple lies and angles, wedge(s), 3 balls for a 30-min session or 6 for a 60-min session. **How.** From your first location, chip until one ball is holed; retire that ball. Move to a new location with the remaining balls (new lie, new distance, ideally varied trajectory); chip until one is holed; retire it. Repeat. The drill ends when you've retired every ball before time expires (or when you give up — both are honest signals). **Time & frequency.** 30 minutes (3 balls) or 60 minutes (6 balls), 1×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: retire all 3 balls within 30 minutes. • Competitive: retire 6 within 60 minutes, including from short-sided and tight lies. **Common mistakes.** Repeating the same easy spot (defeats the variety); skipping to a new location before holing out; using only one club when the lie demands a different shot; not actually setting a time cap (the time pressure is part of the consequence).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Christy Longfield, PGA (Golf.com); mirrors Phil Mickelson's practice$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/intense-practice-game-under-pressure-play-smart/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Pressure Up-and-Down (5-in-a-Row, 3-Miss Restart)$drill$,
 $drill$Get five up-and-downs in a row from varied lies — but rack up three total misses anywhere in the session and the whole thing restarts from zero.$drill$,
 25, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dr. Paul McCarthy (CPsychol, inaugural resident sport psychologist at St Andrews) layers two pressure mechanisms into one drill. First, "in a row" — any single missed up-and-down breaks your streak, so the count restarts to zero on each miss. Second, "three misses total" — accumulate three failures anywhere in the session and you start the whole drill over from scratch. Together they create a tight window: you can survive a streak break, but only a few of them. The drill rehearses exactly the cumulative pressure that turns a 75 into an 80 on the course — one save under pressure is one thing; five in a row with a counter against you is another. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose short game holds up in practice but cracks late in rounds; players preparing for tournaments. Beginners may never see a streak start — keep them off it. **Setup.** A short-game green with varied lies (fringe, light rough, slope, short-sided), a wedge and putter, a way to track streak count and total miss count. **How.** From a chosen lie, play a chip and one putt — making the up-and-down (chip + one-putt or chip-in) counts as a success and adds to your streak. Each missed up-and-down resets the streak to zero AND adds one to your total miss count. Hit 5 successes in a row to complete the drill. If your total miss count reaches 3 at any point, the drill ends and you restart from zero. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes, 1–2×/week on scoring work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete one full 5-in-a-row run. • Competitive: complete it from genuinely varied/short-sided lies. **Common mistakes.** Choosing similar easy lies (kills the variety); conceding the comeback putt (the putt is half the test); abandoning the 3-miss rule when frustrated (the cap is the point); not tracking miss count.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,short_putts,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dr. Paul McCarthy, CPsychol$drill$, 'https://www.drpaulmccarthy.com/post/ten-competitive-pressure-chipping-drills', 'oga', true),

($drill$Elimination Chipping (5-Station Survival)$drill$,
 $drill$Five chipping stations of varied lie and distance — land each chip within six feet of the pin to survive and advance; fail at any station and you're eliminated back to station one.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dr. Paul McCarthy (CPsychol) frames it as a survival format: "5 chip shots from different locations. Must get within 6 feet on each to 'survive.'" Six feet is tight (tighter than the standard up-and-down threshold), and "elimination" means a single fail at any station sends you back to station one. The pressure compounds as you walk to the fifth station with a clean run going — exactly the feel of a late-round par save. By moving through varied lies and distances, the drill ensures you can't lean on one comfortable shape; every station is a different test, and the gate is the same. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players sharpening short-game precision under pressure; players who notice their chipping tightens up over the last few holes. **Setup.** A short-game green; 5 distinct stations placed around it with varied lie (fringe, rough, sand-side, slope, short-sided) and distance (e.g. 5, 10, 15, 20, 25 yards); a wedge and putter; the ability to estimate a 6-ft radius from each pin. **How.** Walk through the stations in order. At each, play one chip. Land it inside 6 feet of the pin → survive and move to the next station. Land outside 6 feet → eliminated; return to station 1 and start over. The drill is complete when you survive all 5 stations in a single attempt. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly on short-game scoring. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: survive all 5 within a session. • Competitive: survive cleanly from tougher stations (short-sided, bunker-adjacent). **Common mistakes.** Setting all stations at similar distances (lose the variety); fudging the 6-ft gate when frustrated; only chipping (the drill is the chip — but the comeback putt is the next drill); skipping the hardest station because you can't get past it (that's where the work is).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dr. Paul McCarthy, CPsychol$drill$, 'https://www.drpaulmccarthy.com/post/ten-competitive-pressure-chipping-drills', 'oga', true),

-- pressure_game · putting
($drill$Golden Eight (Walters)$drill$,
 $drill$Hole three putts in a row from 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 feet — fifteen makes to complete the ladder, and a single miss anywhere drops you back to the start at two feet.$drill$,
 25, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach Ged Walters built this around the "Golden Distance" — the PGA Tour's ~87% make-rate inside 10 feet, the zone where good putters separate themselves from average ones. The drill stations are 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 feet, and at each you must hole three putts in a row before advancing. Walters' rule is unambiguous: "should you miss any putt, start the drill again from two feet." The consequence is total — fifteen makes are required to complete the ladder, and a single miss anywhere wipes everything. Pressure builds painfully as you reach the 8-footer and 10-footer with all progress on the line, which is the entire point: real-round putting is *playing for what you've already built*, not making fresh putts in isolation. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who want a Tour-honest short-putt standard and aren't broken by the restart rule. Not for beginners or casual players — the loop may not close and frustration is the only output. **Setup.** A flat practice green with a hole, tees at 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 feet from the cup, a putter, a few balls. **How.** Start at 2 feet. Hole three in a row → advance to 4 feet. Hole three in a row → advance to 6 feet. Keep going through 8 and 10. Any single miss anywhere → restart from 2 feet, no exceptions. The drill ends when you make all 15 (three at each of the five stations) in succession. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes, 1×/week on short-putt pressure work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete the ladder within a session, even if it takes several attempts. • Competitive: complete it cleanly; consider adding a 12-ft station once the standard ladder is automatic. **Common mistakes.** Cheating the distances; counting non-consecutive makes; abandoning the restart rule when the 10-footer breaks the streak (the breakdown is exactly the rehearsal you need); rushing the short ones because they "should" go in.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Ged Walters, Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/the-average-pga-tour-golfer-makes-87-of-putts-from-inside-10-feet-this-expert-drill-can-help-you-improve-your-success-from-the-golden-distance', 'oga', true),

($drill$Faxon Compass Pressure Drill$drill$,
 $drill$Hit twelve putts from 4, 6, and 8 feet around all four sides of the hole, scoring par on the short ones and birdie on the eights — Tour standard is two-under across the twelve.$drill$,
 20, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** 8-time PGA Tour winner Brad Faxon designed this as a pressure test, not a scoring game — "Can you shoot under par? If you miss one of the shorter shots, can you overcome any anxiety to make an additional eight-footer?" Three balls at each of three distances (4, 6, 8 ft) at each of four compass points around the hole = 12 putts. The 4-footers and 6-footers are "pars" (you must hole them to make par); the 8-footers are "birdies" (a make scores -1). Tour standard is 2-under across the 12 (10 of 12 made); a recreational benchmark is anything under par. The pressure isn't a tracked score you beat next time — it's the threshold itself, plus the fact that a single miss inside 6 feet immediately puts every remaining 8-footer under pressure to claw back to par. The 4-direction layout exposes any directional weakness in your stroke (left-to-right break vs right-to-left). **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with sound mechanics testing themselves; especially players who need to lock in 6-footers under tournament pressure. **Setup.** A flat practice green with a hole; tees at 4, 6, and 8 feet from the cup at four cardinal points (N/S/E/W) around it (12 tees total); a putter; 3 balls. **How.** Walk one cardinal at a time. At the N tee: hit the 4-footer (par if holed), then the 6-footer (par), then the 8-footer (birdie if holed). Move to S, then E, then W. After all 12 putts, total your score relative to par-12. Tour standard: 2-under (≥10 of 12 made). Recreational target: under par. Beat the threshold to "pass." **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: at or under par across the 12. • Competitive: 2-under or better, holing all the inside-6-ft putts. **Common mistakes.** Setting the tees at different distances around the hole (defeats the symmetry); skipping the long ones; not running a routine on every putt (the routine IS the drill at this distance); chasing a personal-best total instead of the pass/fail standard (that turns it into skill_game).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Brad Faxon (Golf.com / Nick Dimengo)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/brad-faxon-pressure-putting-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Putting Ladder (Nicol & Morris)$drill$,
 $drill$Set a semicircle of tees a grip-length behind the hole as a safe zone, then putt from progressively longer stations — short or past the zone and you reset to the first station.$drill$,
 20, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Gary Nicol (PGA) and Karl Morris (renowned mental coach), authors of *The Lost Art of Putting*, built a lag-distance pressure drill that's harsher than it sounds. A semicircle of tees roughly one grip-length (~1 ft) behind the cup defines a "safe zone" between the cup and the back tees. From each station, a putt that holes out OR finishes inside that safe zone counts as success and advances you to the next station. A putt that comes up short of the cup, or rolls past the back tees, resets you to station one. The drill rewards the exact thing tour pros do — die the ball inside a small zone past the cup — and punishes both timid and aggressive lag putts equally. Because the zone is tight, every long station becomes a real speed test under restart pressure. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players whose lag putting leaks strokes; especially players who three-putt either because they leave it short OR blow it past. The safe-zone forgiveness makes it less brutal than the Golden Eight, but the restart still bites. **Setup.** A practice green with room for stations from ~3 to ~15 feet, tees pushed into the green about a grip-length behind a chosen hole forming a semicircle (the safe zone), a putter, a few balls. Set 4–5 stations at increasing distances (e.g. 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 ft). **How.** Start at station 1. Putt one ball: hole it OR stop it inside the safe zone behind the cup → advance to station 2. Short of the cup, or past the back tees → restart at station 1. Continue until you complete the longest station. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1–2×/week on lag work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete the ladder within a session. • Developing: complete it cleanly without resetting on the long station. • Competitive: extend to a 20-ft top station. **Common mistakes.** Making the safe zone too generous; ignoring the "short of the cup" half of the rule (short is the bigger lag fault); cheating the station distances; quitting after one restart at the long station (that's where the drill earns its keep).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Gary Nicol (PGA) & Karl Morris, The Lost Art of Putting$drill$, 'https://www.thelostartofgolf.com/458/', 'oga', true),

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Corpus v2 fill — warmup mode (gradual on-ramp before practice or play —
-- prepare body, tempo, feel, sequence). 9 new, bringing warmup 5 → 14.
-- Sourced + verified 2026-05-28. Boundary enforced: agents cut routines that
-- turned out to be technique drills (Cameron Smith one-armer), pressure games
-- (Mickelson 100-in-a-row), or swing-change protocols (Ruggiero 20-20-20).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- warmup · off_tee
($drill$Five-Ball Driver Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$Tee five drivers and ladder the speed — an easy 100-yarder first, then 125, 150, 175, and a controlled rip — so you arrive on the first tee with the right driver tempo without burning your back.$drill$,
 5, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Scott Munroe built this as the *last* step of the warm-up — the bridge between the range and the first tee. Most amateurs walk to the first hole having either grinded the driver in their final ten range balls (tight, tired, swinging at 100%) or never touched it (cold). Munroe's fix is a graduated five-swing speed ramp: ball position off the lead big toe to encourage the slight upswing catch a driver wants, then build speed across five balls — slow at first to find posture, faster each ball, finishing with one "kill it" swing where you rip without losing balance or posture. The point isn't to grind anything — it's to teach the body that adding speed doesn't mean adding tension. On the first tee you then choose a tempo between balls 4 and 5 (the controlled-fast version), not the max swing. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players whose driver feels different on the course than the range; especially players who arrive at the first tee tight. **Setup.** Tee, driver, 5 balls. **How.** Tee five balls with ball position off the lead big toe. Ball 1 = slow full swing, ~100-yard carry, all focus on posture and finish position. Ball 2 = ~125 yards. Ball 3 = ~150. Ball 4 = ~175. Ball 5 = "kill it" — rip as hard and as far as you can without losing your spikes or your finish. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes — the final block of a warm-up, every pre-round. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: clean contact on 4 of 5, finish balanced. • Developing: ball-flight shape consistent. • Competitive: ball 5's shape matches ball 4's — speed without losing the swing. **Common mistakes.** Adding power instead of speed (causes tension); skipping ball 1 (the slow rep teaches posture); ripping ball 5 with terrible balance (the cap is balance, not yardage); doing it before the rest of the warmup (it's the finisher, not the opener).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Scott Munroe, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/golf-tips-warm-up-your-driver-with-just-5-swings/', 'oga', true),

($drill$First-Hole Dress Rehearsal$drill$,
 $drill$Finish your range warm-up by playing the first hole on the range — your actual tee shot to your actual target, then the approach club you expect — to transfer focus from mechanics to the shot that matters.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teachers Kevin Weeks and Brady Riggs both teach the same closing move on a warm-up: stop hitting "range balls" and start playing your actual scorecard. Weeks's instruction is direct — "play the first hole or two on the range, executing the tee shot they want and then the club they expect to hit into the green." Riggs adds the first par-3's tee shot. The mechanism is transfer: your last few balls happen WITH the same target, pre-shot routine, and shot shape you'll execute on the first tee, so the focus shifts from swing mechanics to "where is this going." It also handles first-tee nerves by rehearsing the literal shot before it counts. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players, especially anyone who tightens up on the first tee or arrives at the course thinking about mechanics instead of targets. **Setup.** Range with a view of the kind of fairway you'll face on hole 1; the actual driver / 3-wood / iron you'll hit off the first tee plus the approach club you typically need. **How.** After the regular tempo and iron build, hit the first-tee club with your full pre-shot routine to a target representing the fairway you'll need. Then hit the approach club you expect into hole 1's green. If your course starts with a par-3 or has an early par-3, hit that tee shot too. 2–3 specific shots, full routine, no rakes. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes — the closing block of every pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: hit the tee-shot target reasonably. • Developing: execute both shots to a real target with your routine. • Competitive: shot shape matches your intent on both. **Common mistakes.** Treating it as just more reps (skip the routine and the transfer is lost); hitting clubs you won't use; not visualizing the actual hole; doing it cold (it's the closer, not the opener).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{course_management,dispersion}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Kevin Weeks & Brady Riggs, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/golf-how-to-warm-up-how-many-range-balls/', 'oga', true),

-- warmup · approach
($drill$7-Iron Calibration Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$Start with the 7-iron — not so short you skip the body warming, not so long you swing tight — and at 70% effort find your low point and calibrate face direction before reaching for anything longer.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher John Dunigan argues the 7-iron is the right warm-up club for adults: a wedge is so short it skips the body-warming benefit, and the driver creates tension before you've moved. The 7-iron sits in the sweet spot — long enough to wake the body up, short enough to find center contact without forcing. Dunigan's protocol starts with a few baseball-style rotational swings (one lefty, one righty) to loosen turn, then 7-irons at 70% effort with one focus: locate the low point of the swing so contact is ball-first, turf-after. Once strikes are clean, he calibrates face direction without changing setup — hit one shot intentionally left of target, one right, one straight, manipulating only the grip and face through the early downswing. Only then does he move to the longer clubs "using a similar process." **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic contact who want a structured on-ramp; beginners should still start with a wedge ladder. **Setup.** Range, 7-iron, no aids. **How.** Baseball swings — a couple lefty, a couple righty — to loosen rotation. Then 7-irons at ~70% effort, focus only on low point (ball, then turf). Once strikes are clean, calibrate face: 1 shot intentionally left, 1 right, 1 straight, varying only grip/face direction. After ~15–20 minutes of 7-iron work, move to the longer clubs with the same easy-effort, low-point-first approach. **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes; every pre-practice or pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean ball-then-turf contact on most 7-irons; recognize left/right shape difference. • Competitive: all three calibration shapes go where you call them. **Common mistakes.** Going to 100% on the first ball; starting with a wedge or driver instead of the 7-iron (loses the calibration value); skipping the baseball swings; rushing past the calibration shapes to "real" shots.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$John Dunigan, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/7-iron-ideal-warm-up-club-john-dunigan/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Spieth Wedge Build-Up (20→40→Full)$drill$,
 $drill$Feet together with the 60° lob, rock the ball 20 yards, then 40, then full — Spieth's wedge primer that extends swing length, not effort, before walking through the rest of the bag.$drill$,
 10, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Jordan Spieth's pre-round wedge build-up — codified by long-time coach Cameron McCormick — is the cleanest published example of "extend length, not effort." Standing feet-together with a 60° lob wedge forces a balanced, body-driven swing (no leg drive to override tempo), and you start by rocking the club back and forth with only enough force to fly the ball about 20 yards. Each subsequent ball flies a few yards farther than the last as swing length, not effort, gradually extends. Over about 7 minutes the wedge stretches from ~20 yards to ~40 yards to a full lob-wedge swing, at which point Spieth progresses up through short irons → mid-irons → long irons → hybrids → woods → driver, picking a real target on every shot and stretching the gap between balls to simulate on-course pace. The whole sequence trains tempo through length, which is exactly the tempo you want on the course. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who lose tempo when they switch clubs cold; players who want a tour-tested pre-round routine. **Setup.** Range, 60° lob wedge to start (or your highest-lofted wedge), then the rest of the bag in order. **How.** Feet together, 60° wedge, ball forward of center. Rock the club back and through with just enough swing length to fly the ball ~20 yards. Each ball, extend the swing length a little, building toward ~40 yards, then full wedge swings. Then progress through clubs: short irons (small handful), mid-irons, long irons / hybrids, woods, finishing with 4–5 drivers. Real target on every shot; stretch the time between balls toward the end to mimic on-course pace. **Time & frequency.** 10–12 minutes for the wedge build-up alone (~7 min wedge, then ~5 min progression); part of a longer warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: wedge yardages step up cleanly without effort changes. • Competitive: smooth tempo carries from the lob wedge through the driver. **Common mistakes.** Adding effort instead of length (kills the point); skipping feet-together for "real" stance early (loses the balance constraint); rushing through balls to get to the driver; finishing with too many drivers (Spieth ends on 4–5, not 20).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Jordan Spieth / Cameron McCormick (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/news/tournaments/jordan-spieth-complete-warmup-minute-by-minute/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Half-to-Full Iron Tempo Ramp$drill$,
 $drill$Stage your iron warm-up by swing length — five or six wedges from hip-high to shoulder-high to full to three-quarter — then move to the mid-iron at the on-course tempo you just found.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Tour player Mark Baldwin (via GOLFTEC) and XXIO golf pro Nathalie Sheehan (golf.com) describe the same canonical pre-round iron sequence: 5–6 wedges progressing by swing length — hip-high → shoulder-high → full → back down to three-quarter — so you finish the wedge block in the swing length you'll actually use on the course. Then 5–6 shots with a 6- or 7-iron, then a few with the longer clubs you'll hit off the first tee. The hip-shoulder-full-three-quarter staging is the key insight: most amateurs jump from half-swings to full and then end on full, but the three-quarter finish is what you actually deliver under pressure on a real shot. Sheehan's framing: it's not technique work, it's "a feel for the day." Baldwin's framing: it's enough reps to find center contact without grinding. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Most universally applicable iron warm-up in the corpus — scales by club choice (any wedge + any mid-iron + a couple of fairway clubs). **Setup.** Range, a wedge, a mid-iron (6 or 7), and the longer clubs you'll hit off the first tee. **How.** Wedges first — 5–6 shots, swing-length sequence: 2 hip-high, 1–2 shoulder-high, 1–2 full, 1 three-quarter finishing shot. Then 5–6 shots with a 6- or 7-iron at the tempo you found. Then a few shots with the longer clubs (hybrid, fairway wood, or driver) — small reps, just to wake them up. Real target on every ball. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes; every pre-round or pre-practice warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean wedge contact at full; mid-iron lands somewhere useful. • Developing: wedge yardages step up by length; mid-iron flight matches what you'll use. • Competitive: the three-quarter wedge feels like an on-course shot. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the half-swings (the body-warming phase); ending on full instead of three-quarter (loses the on-course finish); hitting all the same club to "groove" it (this is warmup, not blocked practice); spending all your range balls on the driver.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Nathalie Sheehan (Golf.com); Mark Baldwin, PGA Tour (GOLFTEC)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/smart-way-maximize-warmup-live-well-play-well/', 'oga', true),

-- warmup · around_green
($drill$Daly Lead-Hand Chip Primer$drill$,
 $drill$Daly's pre-round chip primer — ten to twenty short chips with the lead arm only, fifty-two degree wedge — strips out trail-hand manipulation so the body and tempo are calibrated before the first real shot.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** John Daly's pre-round chip primer has lasted because it does one thing cleanly: it removes the trail hand's ability to flip, scoop, or grab through impact. With the lead arm only, you can't manipulate the clubface — you have to let the clubhead fall onto the ball and use the shoulders and chest to swing through. That's the exact tempo and feel you want from a chip when it actually counts. Daly's protocol is short and specific: a 52° wedge, narrow stance, ball centered with a slight forward shaft lean, and 10–20 small chips with the lead hand only before every round. The drill calibrates strike (the lead arm naturally bottoms out at the same place every time) and primes the body to feel a chip motion rather than a hand motion. **Who it helps most.** Every level — universal warm-up mechanic. Especially useful for players who flip or scoop chips under pressure. **Setup.** A short-game green, a 52° wedge (a 54/56/58/60 also works), 10–20 balls. **How.** Narrow stance, ball centered, slight forward shaft lean. Remove the trail hand from the grip. Swing back and through with the lead arm only, letting the clubhead fall onto the ball and holding the face square (no rollover) through impact. Short chips only — 10–20 balls. Then transition to normal two-handed chips with the same tempo and feel. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes; every pre-round and pre-short-game session. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean contact on most balls; no scoops. • Developing/competitive: tempo and strike pattern carry into the two-handed chips that follow. **Common mistakes.** Trying to swing too far (the lead-only motion is short — long swings break down); rolling the face through impact (the point is to keep it square); using a long iron (the wedge's loft is part of the drill); skipping the transition to two-handed chips at the end (the transfer is the goal).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$John Daly (Wild Golf / Daily Duffer)$drill$, 'https://wildgolf.com/p/best-value-pnw-gold-mountain-golf-olympic-course-bremerton-wa-john-daly-one-handed-drill', 'oga', true),

($drill$Shaw Course-Sim Chip Opener$drill$,
 $drill$A short target-focused opener — five to ten chips from eight to ten yards off the green trying to hole each — that wakes up strike and aim before the round starts.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw, PhD/PGA (Golf Insider), built this as the opening block of a pre-round short-game warm-up. The mechanic is deliberately simple: pick one accessible hole on the practice green from 8–10 yards off, and hit 5–10 chips trying to hole each one. No varied lies, no varied trajectories, no clubs to rotate — one shot, one target, repeated. Shaw's coaching point is that "trying to hole each shot" is a target-focus device, not a scoring goal — the *attempt* to hole out is what tunes aim and strike, even when the ball doesn't fall. The single-shot repetition wakes up the strike quality and aim you need to be sharp on the course without sliding into pressure or scoring. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Strongest fit for beginners and casual players who need a simple, single-shot on-ramp; still useful as the opening block of a developing or competitive player's short-game warm-up. **Setup.** A short-game green, your standard chipping wedge, 5–10 balls, a hole 8–10 yards from a spot just off the green. **How.** Pick the hole and the spot. Hit 5–10 standard chips, trying to hole each one. Same lie, same target, same club — the point is target focus and strike, not variety. **Time & frequency.** 5–10 minutes as the opening block of every pre-round short-game warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean contact on most chips; ball finishes inside 10 ft. • Developing/competitive: most chips finish inside 5 ft; the occasional one falls. **Common mistakes.** Trying to make this a scoring drill (it isn't — the "try to hole each" is a focus device); varying lies or clubs (loses the simplicity); skipping it because it feels too easy (the simplicity is the point — it's the on-ramp); doing it as the only short-game work.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, PGA (Golf Insider)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-warm-up-routine/', 'oga', true),

-- warmup · putting
($drill$Speed Station (15-Inches-Past Lag Primer)$drill$,
 $drill$Stick two tees fifteen inches apart on the practice green, then putt five lag balls trying to leave each one between them — calibrate pace to today's greens without a hole to make or miss.$drill$,
 5, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach Paul Foston designed this around Dave Pelz's "perfect speed for any putt is 15 inches past the hole" principle, applied as a hole-free pace primer. Two tees pushed into the green 15 inches apart create a narrow target zone; you putt 5 balls from a comfortable lag distance trying to leave each one *between* the two pegs. Removing the hole is the key insight — most amateurs roll a few lag putts at the cup before the round, see them lip out or finish 6 feet past, and walk to the first tee with putting anxiety. With no hole, there's no missed putt to negotiate. The narrow zone replaces binary made/missed feedback with graded calibration: between the tees = perfect pace for today's greens; 6 inches off = a small miss; 4 feet off = the speed errors that produce three-putts. Five putts is enough to find the day's pace, fast. **Who it helps most.** Every level. The most universally applicable pre-round putting warm-up — no hole, no break required, just speed. **Setup.** A practice green, 2 tees pushed in 15 inches apart somewhere on a flat section, a putter, 5 balls. Pick a comfortable lag distance (20–30 ft is typical). **How.** Stand at your chosen distance. Putt the 5 balls one at a time toward the tee gate, trying to leave each ball *between* the two pegs. Don't aim at a hole — aim at the zone. The feedback is finishing position relative to the tees: in between = perfect; 6 inches off = adjust slightly; 4+ feet off = today's greens are faster/slower than you thought. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; the opening block of every pre-round putting warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 2 of 5 inside the tee gate. • Developing: 3 of 5. • Competitive: 4 of 5 with no outliers beyond a foot. **Common mistakes.** Aiming at a hole anyway (the point is to remove that feedback); putting from a distance where you can't reach the zone consistently; doing it on broken slope (a flat section is the right surface for pace calibration); skipping it because "I'll feel the speed during the round" (the first hole is too late).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,lag}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Paul Foston, Golf Monthly Top 50 (citing Dave Pelz)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/forget-the-hole-when-putting-on-the-practice-green-try-this-essential-pre-round-drill-instead', 'oga', true),

($drill$Three-Ball Touch Cluster$drill$,
 $drill$Pick a general target on the green ten to fifteen feet away — no hole — and roll three balls trying to leave them touching each other. Tests stroke repeatability cleanly, no read or line distractions.$drill$,
 5, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Monthly Top 25 Coach Andrew Reynolds's three-ball cluster is a stroke-repeatability primer that removes both the cup and any defined zone. You pick a vague general target on the practice green at 10–15 feet (not a hole), roll three balls one after the other, and try to leave all three finishing touching each other. With no hole and no zone, the only feedback signal is whether the three balls *cluster* — which isolates stroke repeatability and tempo independently of read or line. That's exactly what fails after a layoff or in the first 10 minutes of warm-up: stroke length and tempo drift, but you can't see it when you're focused on a hole. Three balls together = stroke is repeatable; three balls scattered = it's not, and you've got 4 sets left to find it. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players; beginners benefit less here (they need explicit target feedback to learn what good contact feels like). Especially useful for players who putt well in practice when the line is obvious but lose stroke quality on real greens. **Setup.** A practice green, 3 balls, a putter, a general target 10–15 feet away (a discolored patch, a divot, an edge — *not* a hole). **How.** Stand at your chosen distance from the general target. Roll the 3 balls one after another, full routine on each, aiming for the same vague target. Walk down and look at where they finished — try to leave all three close enough to touch. Repeat the three-ball set 4–5 times. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; pairs naturally after the Speed Station or as a standalone stroke primer. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: balls cluster within ~2 feet of each other. • Developing: cluster within ~6 inches. • Competitive: three balls genuinely touching. **Common mistakes.** Picking a hole anyway (the cluster is the feedback); aiming at three different targets (kills the variance signal); rushing the three balls (each gets a routine); skipping the walk-down to look at the cluster (the diagnosis is half the drill).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Andrew Reynolds, Golf Monthly Top 25 (Golf Monthly)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/videos/putting-tips/golf-pre-round-putting-drills', 'oga', true),

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Corpus v2 fill — blocked mode (same-shot repetition to hone a TECHNIQUE).
-- 25 new, bringing blocked 19 → 44. Sourced + verified 2026-05-28.
-- Owner boundary: universal techniques with clear "what + when" are IN
-- (toe-down chip, knockdown, gate drill); branded teaching SYSTEMS that need
-- the whole framework are OUT (Short Game Chef, Joe Mayo Spectacular Strike,
-- AimPoint, SeeMore RST). Test: can a player execute from the drill copy
-- alone, or does it require the pro's curriculum?
