-- blocked · off_tee
($drill$Chair-Behind Hips$drill$,
 $drill$Set a chair behind your butt at address and feel both glutes press into it through the swing — if the connection breaks, you've early-extended, which is the root cause of most blocked drives and snap-hooks.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Ludvig Åberg's go-to drill, codified by Golf Digest #1-ranked teacher Mark Blackburn, attacks early extension — the hip-thrust toward the ball in the downswing that costs the room your arms need to deliver the club from the inside. When the hips thrust forward, the body has to make space somewhere, so the arms get stuck (block-right) or flip past (snap-hook) and impact feels armsy. Placing a chair (or couch arm) directly behind the butt at address gives a physical reference: the right glute presses into the chair on the backswing, and the left glute should press back into the chair through transition and impact. The moment butt-to-chair contact breaks, you've early-extended — instant, kinesthetic feedback the player can't argue with. **What it trains.** Posture retention; hip rotation rather than hip thrust. **When to use.** When drives are leaking right (stuck blocks) or snap-hooking; when impact feels armsy/flippy; or as a periodic check on swing posture. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who recognize the fault. Skip for beginners — they don't yet have the diagnosis. **Setup.** A chair, couch arm, or wall behind your butt at address. Driver, no net required — these can be rehearsal swings or hit balls if you have space and a low net. **How.** Address with the chair just touching your butt. Make rehearsal swings (or shots) feeling the right glute press into the chair on the backswing; through transition the left glute should press back into the chair, not pull away. Åberg's pre-shot feel: "pressure shifts from my right heel to my left toe" without the hips thrusting forward. 10–15 rehearsals, then 5–10 shots checking the connection. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly on body work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: maintain butt-chair contact on rehearsals and most shots. • Competitive: ball flight pattern shifts (no more stuck blocks/snap-hooks). **Common mistakes.** Cheating the chair gap; only doing rehearsals (transfer needs reps with a ball); confusing this with a posture drill for the upper body (the hips are the target); rushing the swing.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Ludvig Åberg / Mark Blackburn (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/ludvig-aberg-go-to-swing-drill-fix-early-extension-get-more-shaft-lean', 'oga', true),

($drill$Grass-Skim Sweep$drill$,
 $drill$With the driver set just kissing the tops of the tee-height grass, make swings that only skim the blades — anything deeper means you're hitting down on a club that wants to be hit up.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Digest Best Young Teacher Jason Guss strips driver attack-angle work down to one tactile cue. Most amateurs apply iron mechanics to a teed-up ball and hit slightly down on it — that adds dynamic loft, spikes spin, and kills carry. The fix isn't to "stop hitting down" abstractly; it's to feel a sweep. Setting the driver sole just kissing the tops of tee-height grass turns the grass into a physical reference: swings that only graze the blade tips are sweeping; swings that catch the blades deeper are still descending. Guss frames the feel as "you're sweeping it, and learning to hit up on it instead of hitting down on the ball." **What it trains.** Positive attack-angle grooved into muscle memory without a launch monitor. **When to use.** When drives launch low and spinny, when shots balloon or fall out short, or when face-tape shows contact on the top half (skyball pattern). **Who it helps most.** Beginners through developing players with the descending-strike driver pattern. Competitive players already have positive AoA from launch-monitor calibration. **Setup.** A backyard / tee-box patch of grass roughly tee-height, or a range mat with brushes. Driver, no ball. **How.** Address as you would a teed ball, but with the sole sitting *on top of* the grass blades. Make full swings whose only goal is to skim the very tops of the blades through the impact zone — no deeper. 15–20 rehearsals. When the feel is clear, transition to teed balls applying the same sweep. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 1–2×/week when working trajectory. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: visibly higher launch and more carry on teed drives after a session. • Developing: ball flight matches the "high-launch low-spin" target driver number. **Common mistakes.** Setting the grass too short (no feedback); chopping at the blades to "feel" the swing (that's the wrong motion); skipping the transfer to actual balls; doing it on a mat without grass.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Jason Guss (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/this-is-the-easiest-backyard-driver-drill-weve-ever-seen', 'oga', true),

($drill$Belt-Loop Hip Turn$drill$,
 $drill$Slide an alignment stick through your front belt loops and rotate the backswing until the stick passes a second stick on the ground — forces a real hip turn, not a lateral slide, so the downswing isn't behind schedule.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher John Scott Rattan built a tactile fix for one of the most common amateur faults: sliding off the ball in the backswing instead of rotating. A slide leaves the upper body stacked over the trail leg with no real turn — the downswing then comes from the arms and is always behind schedule. Sliding an alignment stick through the lead-side belt loops gives the hips a visible reference. A second stick on the ground at about a 50° angle (away from target) is the gate. As you swing back, the belt-loop stick has to rotate past the ground stick — which it can only do if the hips genuinely turn behind the ball, recruiting the right hip rather than just the left knee. **What it trains.** Backswing rotation depth (hip turn vs lateral slide). **When to use.** When drives spray both ways inconsistently; when the chest stays over the ball at the top (no real turn); when the player feels like they "slide" off the ball. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose path varies wildly because the backswing turn is incomplete. **Setup.** Two alignment sticks. Push one through the lead-side belt loops so it protrudes toward the target. Lay the second on the ground between your feet at about a 50° angle away from the target line. Driver. **How.** Address. Swing back, rotating enough that the belt-loop stick visibly passes over the ground stick. Trail-foot big toe stays on the ground; head stays tall. Rehearse the turn 5–10 times without a ball, then hit shots while keeping the same rotation depth. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week when working backswing rotation. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: belt-loop stick clears the ground stick on most swings; ball flight tightens. • Competitive: full rotation locked in and reproducible. **Common mistakes.** Sliding the hips off the ball to clear the stick (the rotation is the point); collapsing the trail-foot heel up (lose ground connection); forgetting the head should stay tall and centered; doing it without a club for too long (transfer needs the swing).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$John Scott Rattan, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/alignment-stick-drill-ball-striking-john-scott-rattan/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Block-Behind-Club Push$drill$,
 $drill$Place a block or hardcover book behind the clubhead at address and push it back as you start the swing — primes ground pressure and load instead of the lift-up that costs most amateurs power.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David Leadbetter, with biomechanist Jean-Jacques Rivet (Golf Digest), found that pros press about 125% of bodyweight INTO the ground at the takeaway while amateurs only push about 70% — most golfers actually lighten their ground force by lifting up as they start back, which kills coil and downstream power. The fix is a tactile primer: place a wood block or hardcover book on the ground directly behind the clubhead at address, and push the block backward (away from target) as you take the club away. You can't push the block by lifting up — only by pressing through the feet — so the drill grooves a grounded, loaded takeaway in seconds. **What it trains.** Ground pressure / loading the takeaway; coil. **When to use.** When drives feel weak or short despite full swings; when the player feels like they're lifting up out of posture in the takeaway. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic strike. **Setup.** A wood block, hardcover book, or sturdy object behind the clubhead at address. Driver or 6-iron. **How.** Address. Push the block backward as you start the backswing — feel pressure build under the right heel and the ball of the left foot from the extra effort. Don't lift the chest. Then complete a normal backswing maintaining that grounded feel. 10 rehearsals, then a set of 10 shots applying the feel. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 1–2×/week on power/sequence work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: feel ground pressure increase on the takeaway. • Competitive: distance gains carry into normal swings without the block. **Common mistakes.** Lifting up to "push" the block (defeats the point); pushing only with the arms; skipping the transfer to normal swings; treating it as a once-and-done (it's a feel that erodes — revisit it).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$David Leadbetter / Jean-Jacques Rivet (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/david-leadbetter-power', 'oga', true),

($drill$Three-Shadow-Balls Center$drill$,
 $drill$With the sun at your back, line three balls through your shadow at belt, chest, and head — each part of your shadow should stay over its ball through the swing, exposing sway and reverse-pivots in real time.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Mark Blackburn (Golf Digest #1-ranked teacher) uses your own shadow as a free swing-checker. With the sun at your back, address a ball so your shadow falls clearly in front of you. Place three balls in a straight line through the middle of your shadow: one at your belt buckle, one at the center of your chest, one at your forehead. Now swing. The goal is for each part of your shadow to stay over its corresponding ball — if the belt shadow drifts off the lower ball you swayed; if the head shadow rises off the upper ball you stood up; if the head shadow goes the wrong way you reverse-pivoted. The shadow is a continuous visual record of your turn quality, available the moment you step outside on a sunny day. **What it trains.** Staying centered over the ball through the whole swing (anti-sway + anti-reverse-pivot). **When to use.** When drives are fat/thin/off-the-heel from a swaying low point, or when the player feels off-balance at the top. **Who it helps most.** Beginners through developing players with sway or reverse-pivot patterns; competitive players use it as a periodic check. **Setup.** Sunny day, sun behind you, an open hitting area where your shadow falls clearly. Three balls placed through the middle of your shadow at belt, chest, and forehead levels. Any club (driver fine). **How.** Address with the shadow setup. Make swings — each part of the shadow should stay over its corresponding ball through backswing and downswing. The head may dip slightly below the top ball (that's normal); what you're avoiding is lateral drift. Self-correct, rehearse, then hit balls with the same centeredness. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes whenever the sun cooperates. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: identify which way you sway and stop it. • Developing: shadow holds its ground over all three balls on most swings; ball-strike pattern tightens. **Common mistakes.** Doing it under cloud cover (no shadow, no feedback); standing too far from the balls so the shadow doesn't clearly mark them; freezing to "hold" position instead of swinging naturally; ignoring head drift because it "looks small" (it isn't).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Mark Blackburn (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/Justin-Rose-golf-swing-drill-video-mark-blackburn', 'oga', true),

($drill$Driver Setup Primer$drill$,
 $drill$Tee the ball so two-thirds of it sits above the driver crown, play it forward at the lead toe, drop the trail shoulder slightly — the setup that lets the driver swing up, before the swing even starts.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Tour pro Daniel Berger's pre-shot setup primer (Golf Digest) fixes the address position that quietly sabotages most amateur drivers: a low tee, a centered ball, and level shoulders. Those three together force a descending strike on the one club in the bag that wants to be hit on the upswing. Berger's fix is three specific adjustments — tee so that two-thirds of the ball sits above the crown of the driver, position the ball forward to line up with the big toe of the lead foot, and drop the trail shoulder slightly so the spine tilts away from the target. This pre-loads everything an upward strike needs. Berger's swing thought is to "put the clubface on the back of the ball" so the body doesn't lunge ahead. **What it trains.** Address discipline for the driver. **When to use.** Before any range session with driver, or pre-round; especially when the player tees too low, plays the ball center, or stands tall with shoulders level. **Who it helps most.** Beginners through developing players whose address is fighting the driver before the swing starts. **Setup.** Driver, tees, range. **How.** Tee the ball with about two-thirds of the ball above the crown of the driver at address. Move the ball position forward to align with the lead-foot big toe. Take address, then drop the trail shoulder slightly so the spine tilts away from the target. Rehearse the setup 5–10 times without swinging, feeling each piece. Then hit shots from this exact address. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes; the opening block of every driver session or pre-round. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: setup looks correct from the side (high tee, ball forward, trail shoulder slightly lower) before every drive. • Developing: setup is automatic; ball flight pattern shifts higher and longer. **Common mistakes.** Teeing too low (the most common error); playing the ball center (kills the upward strike); standing level-shouldered (no secondary tilt); rehearsing the setup without ever transferring it to a swing.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Daniel Berger (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/simplify-your-tee-shots', 'oga', true),

-- blocked · approach
($drill$Hogan Towel Connection$drill$,
 $drill$Tuck a small towel high in the lead armpit and hit waist-high iron swings keeping the towel pinned — drops the moment your arms disconnect from your chest, forcing a body-led rotational motion.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Josh Zander refines the classic Hogan/Ballard towel drill: you tuck a small towel high in the lead armpit (or trail armpit for strong-grip players, per Zander's nuance) and make 9-to-3 / waist-high swings with a short iron, keeping the towel pinned the whole time. The towel falls the instant your arms come disconnected from your chest — the moment you start hitting "at" the ball with the hands rather than rotating through it with the body. The constraint forces a body-led, synced motion and a connected takeaway, which is exactly what's missing when iron strikes start drifting toey/heely or feel "armsy" under pressure. Zander and Golf Smart Academy both warn the drill is for short, controlled reps — taking it past waist-height with a full swing breaks the connection by design, so don't try to groove that. **What it trains.** Arm-to-torso connection; body-driven rotation through impact. **When to use.** When iron contact gets toey/heely; when the swing feels armsy; as a pre-round reset for connection. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players whose connection breaks under pressure. **Setup.** A small hand towel, a short iron (8 or 9), range. **How.** Place the towel high under your lead armpit (or trail armpit if you have a strong grip). Take address with the short iron. Make 9-to-3 / waist-high swings — keep the towel pinned through the whole motion. If it drops, you disconnected. Sets of 10 half-swings, then a set of normal-length swings carrying the connection feel without the towel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week or as a pre-round reset. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete 10 swings with the towel staying pinned. • Developing/competitive: connection feel carries into full swings (no towel) with better contact. **Common mistakes.** Taking the swing past waist-height with the towel (kills the drill); gripping tight to "hold" the arms together; doing it only with the towel and never transferring; choosing a long iron (a short iron is the right club).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,tempo}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Josh Zander, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com); Hogan/Ballard lineage$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/right-wrong-way-popular-golf-swing-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Como Chair Drill (Anti-Early-Extension)$drill$,
 $drill$Stand to a chair with the seat facing you and knees about an inch from the seat — swing without your knees bumping the chair to feel posture retention through impact, and kill the hip thrust that causes blocks and shanks.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Chris Como uses a chair as the cleanest physical reference for early extension during iron swings — the hip-thrust toward the ball in the downswing that produces thin shots, blocked rights, and shanks because the body has bailed out of posture. With the chair's seat facing you and knees about an inch from the seat edge, you make backswing-and-through rotations with arms crossed; your knees can't contact the chair if your posture stays. If your knees collapse forward and bump the seat, you've early-extended. An advanced variant moves the knees 4–5 inches back, then teaches a more nuanced sequence: in the downswing the knees move *toward* the chair, then both legs straighten while the upper body stays down, until the knees nearly touch at the finish. **What it trains.** Posture retention through impact; correct lower-body action without hip thrust. **When to use.** When ball flight is thin, blocked-right, or shanky and video shows the hips thrusting toward the ball. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with the diagnosis. Casual players often need a simpler weight-shift fix first. **Setup.** A chair with the seat facing you. Stand to a 7-iron with knees about 1 inch from the seat edge, arms crossed for the basic version. **How.** Make backswing-and-through rotations without your knees touching the chair. Knees collapsing in = early extension. For the advanced version, move knees 4–5 inches back and feel the down-swing sequence: knees move toward chair, then both legs straighten while the upper body stays down, knees nearly touching at finish. Rehearse 10–15 times, then hit shots without the chair carrying the feel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1×/week on posture work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: knees stay off the chair on most rehearsals; ball flight tightens. • Competitive: advanced version's sequence becomes automatic. **Common mistakes.** Confusing this with a weight-shift drill (it's a posture check); doing it only as rehearsals (need transfer to balls); knees bumping the chair and ignoring it; using it without the diagnosis (other faults need other fixes).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Chris Como, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/chair-drill-cure-early-extension-chris-como/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Stinger / Low Window$drill$,
 $drill$Play the ball a ball-width back, set pressure on the lead leg, make a full wide-arc backswing — then de-loft through impact and slow the arms immediately after to keep the flight low.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Sean Foley (Tiger Woods's former coach) breaks down the stinger as a specific technique, not a guess. The ball moves about one ball-width back of normal, pressure sets on the lead leg, hands are ahead at address. Foley's key correction is to make a wide-arc backswing and *rotate fully* — most amateurs try to feel "punchy" by narrowing the arc, which kills control and contact. Through impact, let the trail wrist extend / lead wrist bow (the DJ-style condition) to de-loft the club, then *slow the arms* immediately after impact. The finish stays low because the hands aren't releasing aggressively — the abbreviated, low finish is the result of the slow-arm move, not a manufactured chop. **What it trains.** Low-trajectory iron technique on demand. **When to use.** Into the wind, under tree branches, on links or firm-fairway approaches, or any time you need to take spin off. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic strike. Beginners should learn the standard iron flight first. **Setup.** Range, any mid-to-long iron (5- through 8-iron is the canonical band). **How.** Address with the ball about one ball-width back of normal, pressure on the lead leg, hands ahead. Make a normal wide-arc backswing and rotate fully — don't shorten it. Through impact, feel the trail wrist extend / lead wrist bow to de-loft. Slow the arms right after impact so the finish stays low and abbreviated. Reps with a 6- or 7-iron; 8–10 balls at a time. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week when working trajectory control. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: produce a clearly lower flight than your stock iron with reasonable contact. • Competitive: stinger on demand, controlled curve, useful into-wind option. **Common mistakes.** Narrowing the backswing (kills contact); not de-lofting through impact (the ball just flies normal-high); a normal aggressive release (sends it high again); ball position too far back (causes a chunk or hot-low-screamer).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Sean Foley, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/tiger-woods-stinger-royal-liverpool/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Knockdown 2-2-2$drill$,
 $drill$Grip down two inches, play the ball two inches back, drop the lead foot back two inches — three simple changes that turn any iron into a controlled three-quarter knockdown for wind or trouble.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Tina Tombs's "2-2-2 method" is a knockdown shot you can dial in without rebuilding your swing. Three explicit setup adjustments — grip down 2 inches, play the ball 2 inches back of normal, drop the lead foot back 2 inches into a slightly open stance — combine with a three-quarter back-and-through swing to produce a delofted, lower-flighted, lower-spin shot that finishes in a controlled position. The grip-down and back-of-center ball position deloft the club at impact; the open stance gives the body room to rotate through; the three-quarter swing controls speed and finish length. Reps grooves a reliable wind/trouble shot you can pull off the rack whenever the situation calls. **What it trains.** Reliable three-quarter knockdown — wind, between-club gaps, low-ceiling trouble. **When to use.** Into wind, low-hanging branches, tough lies, or any time you want a controlled "70%" iron flight. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Casuals often struggle to feel a true three-quarter swing — they hit full and decelerate, which is the opposite of the drill. **Setup.** Range, 7-iron through PW. **How.** For each club: grip down 2 inches on the handle, position the ball 2 inches back from normal, drop the lead foot back 2 inches into a slightly open stance. Make a three-quarter back-and-through swing — finish lower than a full swing. Reps in sets of 8–10 per club, focusing on consistent trajectory and a finish length that matches the abbreviated backswing. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly on trajectory and trouble-shot work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clearly lower flight than your stock iron with consistent contact. • Competitive: knockdown distance and trajectory dialed in — known yardage for the controlled flight. **Common mistakes.** Making a full swing (defeats the three-quarter point); not gripping down (no deloft); decelerating into impact (a half-swing back, full-effort through, ends in mishits); finishing high (the low finish matches the low flight).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Tina Tombs, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/master-knockdown-shot-2-2-2-method/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tee-Gate Center-Face$drill$,
 $drill$Plant two tees just wider than your clubhead — toe and heel — and swing a mid-iron cleanly through the gate; clipping either side proves you're missing the center of the face, and you can narrow the gate as strike improves.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Kellie Stenzel's tee-gate drill gives instant heel/toe feedback in a way that face-tape and impact spray can't beat for simplicity. Two tees planted in the ground flanking the ball, with the gate just slightly wider than your clubhead, force the strike toward the center of the face — if you can't pass cleanly through without knocking a tee, you're missing the center. The audible click of a tee being clipped is unmistakable feedback, and you can tell from which side it came whether you're catching the heel or the toe. Stenzel: "If you're unable to do this without hitting either tee, you will likely not hit the center of the face." Tightening the gate as strike improves makes the drill scale with the player. **What it trains.** Center-face strike location (heel-to-toe). **When to use.** When face-tape or impact spray shows scattered strike location; when distance and dispersion are inconsistent despite otherwise-decent contact. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players — universal; gate width scales with skill. **Setup.** Range, a mid-iron, two tees, and the discipline to listen for the click. **How.** Plant the two tees flanking the ball with the gate slightly wider than your clubhead. Hit shots — clean pass through = center contact. A toe click = swing was too far from the ball or face was open; a heel click = swing too close or face shut. Sets of 8–10. Narrow the gate slightly as you improve. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on contact work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: pass through a generous gate on 6 of 10. • Developing: 8 of 10 at a tight gate; flight dispersion tightens. • Competitive: 9 of 10 at a gate barely wider than the clubhead. **Common mistakes.** Setting the gate too wide so you never get feedback; ignoring which side you clip (the pattern is the diagnosis); swinging tentatively to avoid clicks (kills the swing); not tightening the gate as you improve.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Kellie Stenzel, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/how-to-hit-center-clubface-consistently-kellie-stenzel/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Feet-Together Iron$drill$,
 $drill$With feet touching and a 7-iron, make full swings — the narrow base falls you over the moment arms and body disconnect, and Pete Cowen says nine of ten amateurs benefit at the cost of about ten percent distance.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Pete Cowen — long-time coach to Henrik Stenson, Brooks Koepka, and others — uses the feet-together iron drill as a universal balance and sync corrective. Feet touching (or within an inch) gives you a deliberately tiny base. Now make full swings with a 7-iron. The narrow base punishes any disconnect between arm-swing and body rotation — if you swing out of sync, you literally fall over. The drill grooves balance, swing-center stability, and arm-body sync without restricting club speed; you're still making a full swing, you just can't afford to be sloppy. Cowen's framing: roughly 90% of amateurs benefit from this drill, and the trade-off is about 10% distance loss in exchange for major consistency gains. Tee the ball initially for confidence, then hit from the turf. **What it trains.** Balance; swing-center stability; arm-body sync under full swing speed. **When to use.** When ball-flight pattern is two-way (off-balance finishes, varying low-points, "armsy" lunges); a great session-opener. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players — universal. **Setup.** 7-iron, range, balls (tee initially, then turf). **How.** Stand with feet touching or within an inch. Tee the ball at first for confidence; switch to off the turf when balanced. Make full swings — accept the distance loss. Cowen prescribes sets of 10; finish by returning to your normal stance and feeling the difference. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1×/week or as a session-opener. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete sets of 10 without falling; flight tightens. • Developing/competitive: full-speed swings with no balance loss; sync carries into the normal-stance swings that follow. **Common mistakes.** Keeping the swing tentative to stay balanced (defeats the point — go full); spreading the feet slightly to "cheat" the balance; skipping the transfer back to normal stance; treating the distance loss as a problem (it's part of the trade).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Pete Cowen (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/approach-shots/pete-cowen-golf-coach-swing-feet-together-drill/', 'oga', true),

-- blocked · around_green
($drill$Toe-Down Chip$drill$,
 $drill$Stand close enough that the heel of your wedge rides off the ground and only the toe touches — the diagonal leading edge resists digging on tight lies and into-grain, and the stroke is pure putter-rocking.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Phil Mickelson's toe-down chip (Golf.com) is the cleanest answer to the tight-lie problem that wrecks amateurs: a normal chip from a hard, short lie wants to dig, and a dig is a chunk or a skull. Standing closer to the ball so the heel of the wedge rises off the ground and only the toe touches creates a slight diagonal leading edge that resists digging — the club rocks across the surface instead of catching turf. The motion is pure putting: grip and posture mirror your putting stroke, shoulder-rocking, no wrist hinge. Ball pops over the fringe and rolls out like a putt. It's a single self-contained technique with a clear "when" — tight lies, hard ground, into-grain — and it doesn't depend on any larger wedge framework. **What it trains.** Clean contact on tight or into-grain lies by changing the club's effective lie angle. **When to use.** Ball just off the green on a tight lie, or anywhere the grain grows toward you — anywhere a normal chip would dig. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who have a steady putting stroke and can read the popped-out roll. **Setup.** Wedge (any loft works), tight lie just off the green. **How.** Stand closer to the ball than a normal chip — close enough that the heel of the wedge sits off the ground and only the toe makes contact at address. Grip and posture mirror your putting stroke. Make a shoulder-rocking, no-wrist stroke — like a long putt. The ball pops over the fringe and rolls out. Reps: 10–15 chips from a tight-lie spot. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, weekly when working tight-lie short game. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean contact on most reps; ball rolls predictably like a putt. • Competitive: dial in finish distance from tight lies with no chunks or skulls. **Common mistakes.** Adding wrist hinge (kills the putting-stroke simplicity); using it from a cushy lie (the technique solves a tight-lie problem); standing normal distance from the ball (the toe-down setup IS the drill); expecting flight (it's a low roller, not a high stop).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Phil Mickelson (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/phil-mickelson-chip-video-toe-down/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Open-Face Flop (Fowler)$drill$,
 $drill$Open the face before you grip it, set weight forward and ball forward, hold the face open at the top, then commit fully — the high-soft flop for short-sided pins where the ball has to stop on a dime.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Rickie Fowler's flop technique (Golf.com) makes the high-soft stop a repeatable shot rather than a desperate stab. Two non-negotiables: open the clubface *first* and then take your grip — this keeps the face open through the swing, exposing maximum loft and bounce. Set up with weight forward (lead side) and ball forward, and hold the face open at the top of the backswing so bounce stays exposed. Then swing through with weight staying forward and *commit fully* — hesitation kills the drill, producing chunks (you bail at impact) or skulls (you flip to "help" the ball up). Fowler's coaching point is that "trust" is the variable; the technique is sound and the bounce will save you if you commit. **What it trains.** High, soft-landing flop with maximum stopping power. **When to use.** Short-sided pins with no green to work with, when you must stop the ball quickly with little room to roll. **Who it helps most.** Competitive players. It's a high-risk shot; developing players are usually better served by a putt-from-fringe or bump-and-run. **Setup.** Short-game green with a target you can short-side, a high-loft wedge (58° or 60°), 15–25 balls. **How.** Open the clubface to your desired loft *before* you take your grip; grip with the face already open. Address with weight forward, ball forward (off the lead foot), and a slightly open stance. Make a full back-and-through swing — hold the face open at the top, weight staying forward, *commit through impact*. Reps: 10–15 from a good lie focusing on commitment, then 10 from a tight lie. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week when working short-game peak distance. **Success metric.** Competitive: ball lands soft, stops quickly, no chunks or skulls when you commit. **Common mistakes.** Gripping first and then opening the face (the face closes back through impact); decelerating at impact (chunk or skull); shifting weight back to "lift" the ball (the loft does the lifting); trying it for the first time on a critical course shot.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,trajectory}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Rickie Fowler (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/rickie-fowler-tips-hitting-flop-shot/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Bump-and-Run$drill$,
 $drill$Pick a lower-loft iron, narrow stance, ball back to the trail toe, hands ahead — the lowest-risk shot from just off the green, hops on the green early and rolls like a putt.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA professional Brendon Elliott (PGA.com) lays out the bump-and-run as the safety-first short-game shot — a low chip that gets the ball on the ground early and lets it roll out like a putt. Five elements: a lower-loft club (typically a 9-iron or PW), a narrow stance, ball position back toward the trail toe, hands ahead at address to promote a downward strike, and a short, smooth backswing with a matching follow-through. The setup pre-loads the downward strike that catches the ball cleanly and produces low trajectory; once on the ground, the lower loft gives you maximum roll. Compared to a flop or even a higher chip, the bump-and-run has fewer ways to go wrong — fewer chunks, fewer skulls — which is exactly why it's the bread-and-butter shot when conditions allow. **What it trains.** Low-trajectory ball-back chip that hops on the green early and rolls. **When to use.** Tight to the green with green to work with, no hazard to carry, firm conditions — the standard low-risk play. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Strongest fit for beginners and casual players who struggle with higher-loft chips. **Setup.** Short-game green with green to work with, a 9-iron or PW. **How.** Narrow stance. Ball back toward the trail toe. Hands ahead of the ball at address. Short backswing, short matched follow-through, smooth tempo. Don't try to "lift" — the loft on the iron does the lifting; you swing down through the ball. Land the ball a yard or two onto the green and let it roll. Sets of 10. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on short-game. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean contact on most; ball reaches the hole's general area. • Developing: ball lands consistently and rolls to within a few feet. • Competitive: precise distance control. **Common mistakes.** Using too much loft (defeats the purpose); ball position too forward (chunks); hands not ahead (scoops); long, loose backswing (decel comes next); trying to fly it all the way to the hole.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Brendon Elliott, PGA (PGA.com)$drill$, 'https://www.pga.com/story/a-six-step-guide-to-master-the-bump-and-run-chip', 'oga', true),

($drill$Dan Grieve Tee-Peg Pitch$drill$,
 $drill$Tee a ball at driver height with a 54° or 56° wedge — try to clip the ball cleanly without disturbing the tee, contacting the third or fourth groove for a shallow high-spin strike instead of a dig.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Master Professional Dan Grieve (Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach) built a constraint drill that forces a shallow, "clipping" pitch strike instead of the digging chunk that ruins most amateur pitches. You tee a ball at driver height, rest it on the tee, and hit it with a 54° or 56° wedge. The instruction is to hover the club at the ball's base and try to clip the ball off the tee *without disturbing the tee*. The contact should be on the third or fourth groove of the face — meaning you've brushed the ball cleanly rather than driven down into it. The drill is brutal-but-clean feedback: knock the tee and you dug; clip cleanly and you'll see a higher-spin pitch leave the face. Repetition grooves the shallow strike for 20–60 yard pitches from a good lie. **What it trains.** Clipping the ball (vs digging) on pitch shots — high-spin contact via the third/fourth groove. **When to use.** 20–60 yard pitch shots from a good lie where you want height and spin rather than a divot-driven flighted shot. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who chunk pitches or struggle to put spin on the ball. **Setup.** A short-game green or range, a 54° or 56° wedge, tees, balls. Tee balls at driver height with the ball resting on the tee. **How.** Hover the wedge at the ball's base (level with where you'd normally address). Make a pitch-length swing trying to clip the ball off the tee without knocking the tee over. Contact target: the third or fourth groove of the face. Sets of 10. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly when working pitching. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clip without disturbing the tee on most balls. • Competitive: every shot leaves the tee standing, ball flies high and spins. **Common mistakes.** Teeing too low (no constraint); hitting down on the tee (defeats the point); rushing without checking the tee after each shot; using a 60° (the loft makes it easier — Grieve specifies 54° or 56° because they're the more common pitch clubs).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,trajectory}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Dan Grieve, PGA Master Pro (Golf Monthly)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/this-is-my-favourite-drill-dan-grieve-shares-the-secret-to-hitting-better-pitch-shots', 'oga', true),

($drill$Uphill Bunker Slope-Match$drill$,
 $drill$Align your shoulders to the upslope so the club chases the slope instead of digging, play the ball forward, dig your feet in — expect a high short flight, and step down a club for longer carries instead of swinging harder.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Master Professional Kellie Stenzel (Women's Golf) teaches uphill bunker shots as a matter of matching the body to the slope rather than fighting it. Align your shoulders to the slope — this effectively adds loft and allows the club to chase up the slope instead of digging into it. Play the ball forward in the stance, dig your feet in for footing, make a regular swing, and keep the legs quiet so the club can follow the slope. Trying to lift the ball or muscle it up actively makes it worse because it forces the club down into the sand. The result is a high, short flight that stops quickly — and for longer carries, the answer is to drop down a club rather than swinging harder. **What it trains.** Bunker technique on upslope lies. **When to use.** Greenside bunker with an upslope between you and the green / pin. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Beginners need flat-lie bunker mechanics first (Bunker Sand-Entry, Bunker No-Ball Splash). **Setup.** A greenside bunker with an upslope, a sand/lob wedge. **How.** Address. Align shoulders to the slope (left shoulder higher than right for a right-hander). Play the ball forward in the stance. Dig your feet in for solid footing. Make a regular swing — keep the legs quiet and let the club chase up the slope. The ball comes out high and short. For longer carries, take a less-lofted club; do not swing harder. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, monthly bunker work or whenever an upslope is on the practice agenda. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean splash from upslope lies, ball reaches the green. • Competitive: distance control by club selection. **Common mistakes.** Staying level-shouldered (kills the splash, leaves you in the bunker); trying to lift the ball; swinging harder for longer carries (use a less-lofted club instead); ball position too far back.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Kellie Stenzel, PGA Master Pro (Women's Golf)$drill$, 'https://womensgolf.com/uphill-lie-bunker/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Downhill Bunker Slope-Match$drill$,
 $drill$Match knees, hips, and shoulders to the downslope, hinge steeply for a steep attack (downhill kills loft), keep the loft on low through impact — accept a lower flight with more roll and play for the up-and-down, not the pin.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Craig Fricker (National Club Golfer) teaches the downhill bunker shot as the mirror of the uphill — match the body to the slope rather than fighting it. Knees, hips, and shoulders all tilt to follow the downslope, which naturally moves more weight to the lead side. Because a downhill lie kills loft, the answer isn't to add it through wrist action (which produces chunks); the answer is a steep wrist hinge on the backswing for a steep, abbreviated attack that delivers the club at the right angle on the slope. Through impact, "keep the loft on low" — don't let the club release past the hands the way you would on a flat-lie splash. The realistic target is an up-and-down, not a tight pin; you'll get a lower flight with more roll, and that's correct. **What it trains.** Bunker technique on downslope lies. **When to use.** Greenside bunker with the ball on a downslope. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. **Setup.** A greenside bunker with a downslope, a sand/lob wedge. **How.** Address. Match knees, hips, and shoulders to the slope angle (weight ends up on the lead side as a result). Hinge the wrists steeply on the backswing to create a steep attack angle. Through impact, keep the hands ahead and "the loft on low" — do not flip or release past the hands. Expect a lower flight with more roll. Pick a landing spot well short of the pin. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes when downhill bunker is on the practice agenda. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean splash, ball gets out of the bunker. • Competitive: ball-out percentage near 100%; distance control by landing spot. **Common mistakes.** Trying to add loft by flipping (chunks); standing level (kills the splash); not hinging steeply enough (shallow attack on a downhill = thinning); aiming for the pin (the geometry usually won't allow it — aim for the up-and-down).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Craig Fricker, PGA (National Club Golfer)$drill$, 'https://www.nationalclubgolfer.com/golf-tips/short-game/golf-tips-downhill-bunker-shot/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Belly-Wedge from the Collar$drill$,
 $drill$Grip a wedge like your putter, hover the leading edge at the ball's equator, and use a putting stroke to deliberately thin it — the leading-edge contact rolls the ball forward when a putter would snag and a chip would chunk.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Stan Utley (SwingU Master Faculty, Golf Digest Top 50 in America) teaches the belly wedge as the answer to the cut-line problem: the ball sitting on the boundary between fringe and rough, where a putter snags on the longer grass and a normal chip risks a chunk or skull off the awkward lie. The trick is to use the wedge's leading edge with a putting stroke — grip the wedge the way you grip your putter, hover the leading edge level with the equator (middle) of the ball, and use your normal putting stroke. The contact deliberately "thins" the ball with the leading edge, which creates top-spin and a controlled, putting-style roll. Because you're hovering, the bounce slides over the grass behind the ball rather than catching it. It's a single self-contained technique with a clear "when" — collar lies. **What it trains.** Putt-with-a-wedge escape technique for fringe/collar lies. **When to use.** Ball sits on the cut line between fringe and rough, or against the collar — putter won't slide through cleanly but the lie isn't bad enough for a true chip. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who get caught in awkward lies. **Setup.** A spot on the practice green with fringe and rough adjacent (or any spot where the ball can rest against longer grass). Wedge (sand or pitching). **How.** Grip the wedge with your putting grip. Take your normal putting posture. Hover the leading edge of the wedge level with the equator of the ball (NOT behind the ball on the ground). Make your normal putting stroke and strike the ball at its middle — deliberately thin it with the leading edge. The ball rolls forward like a putt. Reps: 10–15 from a collar lie. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes when working short-game escapes. **Success metric.** Ball rolls cleanly off the leading edge, like a putt. **Common mistakes.** Trying to make a chipping motion (you'll catch the grass); not hovering the club (defeats the slide-over-grass benefit); aiming below the ball's equator (chunk it); using too much swing length (it's a putt-stroke).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Stan Utley (SwingU)$drill$, 'https://clubhouse.swingu.com/instruction/belly-your-wedge-to-escape-this-tricky-spot-2/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Putt-from-Fringe$drill$,
 $drill$Putt across the green to the far fringe and back as a pace primer — Shot Scope data says scratch golfers go 81% up-and-down putting from fringe vs 8–22% wedging, and the under-used putter is the highest-percentage play whenever the lie's clean.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game,putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA professional Neil Marr (Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach), drawing on Shot Scope's tour-of-millions-of-shots dataset, makes the data-backed case that amateurs dramatically under-use the putter from off the green. The numbers: scratch golfers get up-and-down 81% of the time putting from the fringe vs 8–22% with any wedge; a 25-handicap goes 62% up-and-down putting vs 21% / 22% / 9% / 8% with PW/GW/SW/LW. The technique itself is simple — three questions decide whether to putt: can the putter strike the ball cleanly through any grass behind it, how much fringe (5+ ft is hard to judge), and what's the fringe condition? If you green-light it, take a normal putt from the fringe distance and add a touch more pace to account for deceleration crossing the fringe. The drill builds the speed feel: putt balls from one side of the green across to the opposite fringe (no cup) and back, building fringe-pace feel without scoring noise. **What it trains.** The default fringe-putt mechanic and fringe-distance pace feel. **When to use.** Ball on or just off the fringe with a clean lie, no significant fringe hump, less than 5 ft of fringe to cross. Don't use when there's long grass behind the ball or undulating fringe. **Who it helps most.** Every level — the Shot Scope data argues all skill levels under-use the putter from the fringe. **Setup.** A practice green with accessible fringe, putter, balls. **How.** Place several balls along one side of the green. Putt each to the opposite fringe (no cup), then putt them back. Add a touch more pace than you'd use on the green to account for fringe deceleration. Rotate distance, repeat. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: balls reach the far fringe consistently. • Developing: balls finish within a few feet of the far fringe. • Competitive: tight pace control across varying distances. **Common mistakes.** Putting through long grass that snags (use a wedge instead); ignoring fringe condition; not adjusting pace for the fringe crossing; defaulting to a wedge from a clean lie because "that's what you do."$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,lag}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Neil Marr, PGA (Golf Monthly); Shot Scope dataset$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/when-should-i-putt-from-the-fringe-this-data-will-challenge-your-short-game-mindset', 'oga', true),

-- blocked · putting
($drill$Chalk-Line Start Line$drill$,
 $drill$Snap a chalk line along a straight 20-foot putt and roll balls with their painted line aligned to the chalk — the ball's stripe should roll end-over-end along the chalk, and any drift tells you exactly how the face missed.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Bryson DeChambeau's chalk-line start-line drill (Golf.com) is the most honest feedback available for putter-face control. Snap a chalk line on a relatively straight ~20-foot putt and align each ball's painted line to the chalk. As you roll the ball, the stripe traces a continuous record of its path — if the stripe stays on the chalk, the face was square at impact; any drift left or right shows exactly how much the face missed and in which direction. DeChambeau: it lets him "calibrate my start line, so I know the ball is rolling off the face the way I want it to." Unlike a gate drill (pass/fail at one point) or a tee track (constrains start direction), the chalk line shows the entire roll — the diagnosis is continuous and unambiguous. **What it trains.** Face-square contact + start line — proof that the ball actually starts where you aimed. **When to use.** When putts that look pure miss right or left of the intended line — diagnostic for face-control errors invisible at address. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Beginners get little signal — they need solid contact first. **Setup.** A practice green with a relatively straight ~20-foot section, chalk, line snapper (or a string and chalk), a putter, balls with painted alignment lines. **How.** Snap a chalk line along the straight putt line. Set each ball with its painted line aligned to the chalk. Roll putts — watch whether the stripe rolls end-over-end ALONG the chalk. Drift left = pull/closed face; drift right = push/open face. Reps: sets of 5–10. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on stroke work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: most balls track the chalk for the first several feet. • Competitive: balls track the chalk for the entire roll. **Common mistakes.** Doing it on a breaking putt (the chalk only works on a straight line); not aligning the painted ball line to the chalk; ignoring the drift pattern (the drift is the diagnosis); rolling too fast (the chalk smudges quickly — use one or two rolls per chalk pass).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Bryson DeChambeau (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/pre-round-drill-us-open-champ-sync-up-putting/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Eye-Drop Setup Check$drill$,
 $drill$Take your putting setup, hold a second ball at the bridge of your nose, and drop it — if it lands on the address ball your eyes are over the ball, outside means too far away, inside means too close.$drill$,
 5, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods's eye-drop check (documented by Golf Digest at the 2023 Masters) tests the single setup precondition for being able to see the line accurately: are your eyes directly over the ball? With eyes off the ball, you're looking at the target line from an angle that distorts both direction and distance — you can have a great stroke and still putt to the wrong spot. The test is dead simple: take your normal putting address over a ball, hold a *second* ball up to the bridge of your nose, and drop it. If your eyes are correctly positioned, the dropped ball lands on top of (or just inside) the address ball. Lands outside = you're standing too far from the ball; lands inside = too close. Adjust posture/distance, repeat. Tiger drops 5 times before he hits a putt — about two minutes, no strokes. **What it trains.** Eye position at address. **When to use.** Before any putting session or pre-round when alignment feels off; when you keep starting putts on lines you didn't intend; periodic setup recalibration. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players — universal. Beginners can use it; they just won't always know which adjustment to make. **Setup.** A practice green, a putter, two balls. **How.** Address a ball on the green as you would for a real putt. Hold the second ball at the bridge of your nose, just under your eyes. Drop it. Note where it lands relative to the address ball. Adjust posture / distance from the ball if the dropped ball lands outside or inside, and repeat. Tiger's prescription: 5 drops before each putt during a setup-check session. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; before any putting practice or as a pre-round check. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: dropped ball lands on or near the address ball most reps. • Developing/competitive: dropped ball lands precisely on the address ball every time. **Common mistakes.** Holding the second ball too far from your nose (artificial position); skipping the adjustment after a miss; doing it without an actual putt afterward (no transfer); thinking eye position is "set forever" (it drifts).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Tiger Woods (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/tiger-woods-masters-2023-putting-drill-eyes', 'oga', true),

($drill$Chopsticks Connected-Stroke$drill$,
 $drill$Place an alignment rod under each forearm with the rods crossing at your sternum — the rods press on top of both arms and physically constrain the wrists, so the stroke becomes body-driven and connected.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Kevin Sprecher's "chopsticks" drill, which he credits to Dr. Craig Farnsworth, is the cleanest physical reference for a connected, body-driven putting stroke. You place one alignment rod under each forearm so each rod runs across the forearm and extends out behind you. The lead-arm rod crosses *over* the trail-arm rod at your sternum, forming an X. Your hands grip the putter normally, but the rods press lightly on the top of both forearms at the same place. Now you can't independently flick the wrists or break the arm triangle without dislodging the rods — the constraint forces the arms to move *together* with the torso, killing wrist independence and shoulder/arm disconnection. Sprecher: it "gives you the sensation of being more 'connected' in the stroke and reduces excessive wrist motion … feel you are using your body more and hands less." **What it trains.** A connected, body-driven stroke; eliminates wrist independence. **When to use.** When putts feel handsy or jerky; when stroke quality breaks down under pressure; when distance control is inconsistent across short sessions (a sign of wrist independence). **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players. Beginners need a baseline stroke first; this drill diagnoses wrist independence in an existing stroke. **Setup.** A practice green, putter, two alignment rods. **How.** Hold both rods. Slide the trail-arm rod under your trail forearm. Slide the lead-arm rod under your lead forearm so it crosses *over* the trail rod at your sternum (forming an X). Grip the putter normally — hands under the rods, rods pressing lightly on both forearms at similar points. Make putting strokes. The rods constrain the arms to move together with the torso. Sets of 10–15 strokes; then remove the rods and putt 5 balls trying to preserve the connected feel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on stroke work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: rods stay in place across full strokes; feel of "body, not hands." • Developing/competitive: connected feel transfers to rod-free strokes; distance control improves. **Common mistakes.** Gripping too tight to hold the rods (kills feel); not crossing the rods (loses the constraint); skipping the rod-free transfer putts; expecting it to fix line/aim (it fixes connection — pair with start-line work).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Dr. Craig Farnsworth / Kevin Sprecher, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/simple-putting-drill-reduce-wrist-motion/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tee-in-the-Cup Speed Pair$drill$,
 $drill$Push a tee into the back lip of the cup and try to hit it on uphill 5-to-10-footers — then move the tee to the front lip and try to drop downhillers in without touching it. Uphill firmness, downhill touch, two paces, one drill.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Brad Faxon (8-time PGA Tour winner, considered one of the best putters ever and Rory McIlroy's putting coach) uses a single tee in the cup to train two opposite paces in one short drill. Uphill set: push a tee into the back lip of the cup so it stands about an inch above the surface. From ~5–10 feet straight uphill, roll putts trying to *hit* the tee on the way in — forces enough firmness that the ball won't die on the lip. Downhill set: move the tee to the *front* lip; from straight downhill the same distance, roll putts trying to drop them in *without* touching the tee — forces a soft, dying-speed roll. Faxon's exact direction: "On uphill putts, put a tee in the back of the hole … hit the ball hard enough so that your ball will hit that tee on the uphill putt. Now on the downhill putt, it's the opposite … I want the ball to roll in and not touch the tee." The pair builds firmness-vs-touch as opposite skills, each with a binary make-or-fail signal. **What it trains.** Speed control on slope — uphill firmness vs downhill touch. **When to use.** When uphill putts come up short or downhill putts run by; when speed control on sloped greens is the persistent leak. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players who have access to a sloped practice green. Beginners can do it but speed-on-slope is a higher-level diagnostic. **Setup.** A practice green with a clear slope and at least one hole on it, a tee, putter, balls. **How.** Uphill: push tee into the back lip of the cup so it stands ~1 inch above the surface. From 5–10 feet straight uphill, roll putts trying to *hit* the tee on the way in. 5–10 reps. Downhill: move tee to the front lip. From straight downhill 5–10 feet, roll putts trying to drop them in *without* touching the tee. 5–10 reps. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: hit the back tee on most uphill makes; don't touch the front tee on most downhill makes. • Developing: separation between paces is clean. • Competitive: both paces dialed and repeatable. **Common mistakes.** Setting the tee on flat ground (the slope is half the drill); choosing too long a distance (10 ft max); chasing one pace while neglecting the other; not pairing them in the same session.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Brad Faxon (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/one-best-ever-putters-placing-tee-cup-why/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Acceleration Coin$drill$,
 $drill$Balance a coin on the putter's back cavity and stroke — the coin should fly off backward at the transition from backstroke to forward stroke, proving peak speed at the START of the forward stroke (the pro pattern), not at impact (the jab).$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Phil Kenyon (Rory McIlroy's putting coach, GOLF Top 100 Teacher) uses a coin on the putter's back cavity to give immediate visible feedback about *when* peak acceleration happens in the stroke. The good-putter pattern is peak putter-head speed at the *start* of the forward stroke (right at the transition from backstroke to forward stroke), with the head decelerating smoothly into impact. The amateur fault is the opposite — slow back, slow forward, then jab at the ball — which produces hits, mishits, and short-putt yips. Balance a coin on the back cavity of the putter. Take your normal stroke. The coin should fly off the putter *backward* (away from the target) right at the transition — that confirms peak acceleration happens at the start of the forward stroke. If the coin only falls near impact, you're accelerating too late (jabbing). If it falls during the backstroke, your takeaway is too fast. *(Despite the drill being from Kenyon, no Kenyon framework or vocabulary is needed — the coin gives all the feedback you need.)* **What it trains.** Tempo and acceleration timing in the putting stroke. **When to use.** When you decelerate into impact (the classic short-putt yip-precursor); when the stroke jabs rather than rolls; when tempo feels rushed. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with a coachable baseline stroke. **Setup.** A practice green, a putter (mallet works best — flat back cavity holds the coin reliably; many blades work too), balls, a small coin. **How.** Balance the coin on the back cavity of the putter. Take a normal stroke. Watch where the coin flies off — backward at transition (correct), at impact (late = jab), during the backstroke (takeaway too fast). Adjust tempo, repeat until the coin reliably ejects backward at transition. Sets of 10 stroke-only reps; then putt 5 balls trying to reproduce the feel without the coin. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 1×/week on tempo work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: coin ejects backward at transition on most strokes. • Competitive: every stroke ejects the coin backward; strokes without the coin feel acceleration-at-transition. **Common mistakes.** Using a blade with no back cavity (coin won't balance); coin too small (no signal); skipping the transfer to balls; treating it as a once-and-done (the feel erodes).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Phil Kenyon (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/10-pga-tour-player-approved-putting-drills-you-can-try/', 'oga', true),

